window.harnessChunks["rows-eff13f596cefac6d.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T22:25:03+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Sinkear/agentwork\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agentwork</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Sinkear/agentwork/tree/3aecbef55ac694d64cb77403092aa850279b60a8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3aecbef55ac694d64cb77403092aa850279b60a8\">3aecbef &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: CLI-model execution of assigned goals and delegated sub-goal code/work tasks, with context and tool-result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing repo contents, requirements, test failures, independent reviewer verdicts, integration-base shifts, consulted advice and human rejection reasons. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose substantive work/edits/tool calls and responses in owner/sub-goal runs; local assignee chooses how to repair a rejected or conflicting revision. - Decision owner: External connected CLI model running under first-party agentwork role/identity token as an explicit operational actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Daemon/machine dispatcher, per-run worktree, assigned agent persona, ACP/JSONL/JSON-RPC transport, role-scoped CLI, timeouts/watchdogs, stateful resume and Git worktree snapshot. - Closure path: Human goal → owner run/model plans/directs → assignee model executes local project changes and reports → run terminal/verification signals → feedback/wake/context into successor agent turns → revised task or completion. - Boundary reachability: Documented agentwork connect launches configured agent CLI runtimes on machine dispatch, and the first-party service creates owner/sub-goal runs with explicit role context. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The models decide which sub-goals to create, tools to invoke and code changes to make; deterministic state reconciliation and permission checks are support, not task reasoning. - Evidence: README quick start, run prompt assembly, sub-goal creation. - Basis: explicit + structural first-party operational integration. - Confidence: High for implemented role/feedback reachability, medium for live external-model behavior. - Caveats: No authenticated target-domain agent process was launched in this review; internal behaviors of ACP/CLI backends are not imported.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Detect and attenuate conflicts between independently produced sub-goal changes, directing the responsible assignee to rebase/rework rather than silently integrating an incompatible revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different S1 sub-goal units edit against a shared goal branch and create incompatible integration bases or merged changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A failed owner integration marks a specific Change conflicting, invalidates its prior verified state and enqueues the same assignee on the latest goal base; owner later chooses integration of a new ready revision. - Decision owner: First-party constructor-owned service transition and event/goal coordinator establish the S2-specific return, with model owner initiating integration and assignee choosing the concrete repair. No independent agent-owned S2-specific attenuation policy is claimed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-run isolated branches/worktrees, Change and ordered revisions, integratechange, conditional transitions, qualityiteration and idempotent run enqueue. - Closure path: Parallel sub-goal outputs → owner integrates one into shared branch → subsequent integration conflict → service marks conflict and enqueues affected assignee → assignee reworks against newer base → new revision becomes ready → owner retries integration. - Distinct S1 units: Concurrent independently assigned sub-goal implementation runs, each with its own assignee/worktree and Change against the same goal branch. - Inter-S1 disturbance: A previous sub-goal&#39;s integration changes the shared base, making another independently completed change incompatible at merge. - Attenuating coordination relation: Conflict-specific MarkChangeIntegrated(false) returns the disrupted sub-goal to rework instead of accepting stale output; Change revision history preserves the old failure/new base relation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The assignee receives a successor run and prompt text explicitly identifying the prior conflicting Change and requirement to resolve it on the new integration base. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: A concrete disturbance is generated between independently produced S1 changes, then causes a targeted adjustment in the affected peer&#39;s subsequent work; static worktree isolation alone is not the positive witness. - Boundary reachability: The documented sub-goal/change-integration workflow invokes MarkChangeIntegrated on failed Git integration, with the recovery path wired into normal owner/assignee runs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The relation is first-party fixed conflict policy (C), while agent workers provide the actual revision and owner chooses when/what to merge; no general independent S2 judgment actor is shown. - Evidence: collaboration conflict contract, conflict transition/rework, assignee conflict wake. - Basis: explicit + structural constructor control with subsequent operational return. - Confidence: High for specific first-party conflict-loop mechanics. - Caveats: C does not assert a free-standing model coordinator that negotiates all possible peer conflicts; ordinary consult/handoff alone are not counted as S2.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Own and regulate goal-wide current commitments — sub-goal assignment, integration/recovery and handover — with a separate legitimate human intervention mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing incomplete sub-goals, failed verifications, ready/conflicting changes, stalled owner runs, disputed acceptance and human-requested rework. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent owner may choose assignees/sub-goals, request help, transfer ownership and integrate ready changes from a current goal-wide state; parent human may redirect/assign and reject a goal in review, returning binding correction to the owner. - Decision owner: Agent/squad leader owner model in autonomous mode (A) under goal-role authority; human operator acting through review/assignment UI/API in parent mode (P). Coordinator derives attention but does not make these substantive decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Goal/sub-goal/change/verification SQLite state, role tokens and requireOwnerOf, owner-only CLI, event-driven OwnerAttention, guarded single owner run, human review state and feedback comments. - Closure path: Goal/sub-goal/change status → derived owner attention and current context → owner model allocates or integrates operational commitments → assignee execution and results → persisted state and next owner wake. Parent mode: goal-wide review evidence → human approve/reject/redirect → binding review transition → a rejected goal creates a new owner run with explicit human reason, or approval triggers delivery. - Whole-system current view: The owner can list goal/sub-goals/changes/verification and sees an actionable wake summary; human UI exposes goal progress, review and changed artifacts. This is the whole declared goal, not an unverified cross-domain fleet command center. - Current-control decision scope: Assigning work to specific S1 units, integrating/reworking their results, changing owner accountability, deciding continuation vs accepted delivery and returning human rejection priorities into next operation; distinct from static concurrency caps. - Boundary reachability: README and Collaboration.v2.md prescribe owner-only subgoal/integration/handoff tools; service rejects non-owner use, wakes owner after actionable changes and records human review decisions via ResolveReview. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The connected owner model owns substantive task division and integration decisions in agent-assigned mode. The human&#39;s accepted/rejected review and reassignment are distinct authorized parent interventions; the daemon only enforces transitions.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Separately assigned agent verifier checks a sub-goal&#39;s actual output from an independent stable snapshot and can reject work before owner integration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: An assignee reports completed while produced code/result violates the sub-goal&#39;s declared requirements or quality expectations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Named verifier agent issues structured passed or rejected with evidence through verifysubgoal; rejected verdict prevents Change admission and enqueues another assignee run. - Decision owner: Explicitly configured distinct verifierid model acting in role=verify; platform authenticates verdict to that named verifier and owns deterministic state transition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Detached Git snapshot for verifier, separate verify run identity, no owner role authority, persisted verificationresult, agent-specific verdict tool and assignee rework loop. - Closure path: Assignee completes and source revision is frozen → verification run inspects stable snapshot → verifier model returns verdict/evidence → platform records result → rejected reopens task and enqueues the assignee with rejection summary, passed creates a ready Change for owner. - Claim being audited: The assignee&#39;s produced sub-goal deliverable is complete/valid enough to become an integrable Change. - Ordinary reporting path: Assignee&#39;s run completion, summary, commits and domain machine-check output recorded on the goal and sub-goal. - Complementary access path: Dedicated verifier run in a detached snapshot of the submitted sub-goal branch can inspect real work/files and issue independent verdict/evidence; named verifier&#39;s verifysubgoal call, not assignee self-report, closes the audit. - Independence boundary: Distinct configured verifier agent/worktree/run/role versus assignee, with a stable detached submitted ref. Service verifies verifier identity, status and running verdict window; a single-actor configuration or default machine verification does not demonstrate independent agent audit. The code validates the named verifierid but does not categorically forbid configuring it equal to assignee ID, so independence must be configured. - Who acts on findings: Service immediately transitions rejected sub-goals into new assignee work with qualityiteration increment and rejection summary injected; passed work goes to owner integration. Squad reviewer comments are separately human-advisory and are not the source of this positive claim. - Boundary reachability: The subgoal API supports verifierid, daemon dispatches role=verify on a stable source snapshot and VerifySubGoal commits the verdict plus subsequent correction run. This is a first-party standard optional mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model verifier decides the substantive completion judgment on independent source evidence; routine machine verification/guardrails are supporting checks only. - Evidence: sub-goal creation/verifier ID, verdict authority and rework, detached verifier workspace. - Basis: explicit + structural complementary audit mode. - Confidence: High for first-party configured verifier control, medium for actual independence when an operator does not select distinct agents. - Caveats: Default verifier is machine-command-based and not a positive agent S3 by itself; human review of squad opinions remains the human&#39;s verdict. Role-level sandbox relies on first-party worktree/role implementation and external execution environments.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No established agent-owned or parent-closed prospective adaptation of the domain/goal organization&#39;s future capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New tracker issues, schedules, acceptance failures and historical task results affect current/recurring work, not a decision on future organization design. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human defines domain policy and runtime/agent configuration; a processor can compile natural-language acceptance rules, but has no authority to choose prospective strategic changes in a future environmental model. - Decision owner: Operator for domain acceptance/agent roster; active agents for present work only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Policy-text compilation to deterministic checks, history, gate statistics, cron/issue triggers and domain reporting. - Closure path: Human policy → checks/gates → operational verification/delivery; schedule → clone current goal. Neither is environmental futures → adaptation options → strategic selection → reconfiguration return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Acceptance-policy compilation and recurring triggers are present control configuration, not prospective intelligence about external future requirements. - Evidence: policy and trigger overview, domain policy configuration. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A human may use historical results to redesign future capabilities outside this bounded first-party loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No declared closed ultimate-purpose/identity arbitration authority beyond human domain acceptance and operational gates. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unapproved side effects, assignee role violation, poor task quality and agent handoff loops are bounded by service/human gates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human may define domain acceptance, approve/reject a goal or stop a handoff loop; those are goal acceptance/operational continuation decisions rather than enduring identity/purpose arbitration. - Decision owner: Single human operator for policy configuration and review actions; deterministic service for permission checks. No recurring S5 issue adjudicator distinct from S3 control. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Goal ownership authority, role tokens, acceptance-policy freeze, human checkpoint, handoff-loop guard and audit logs. - Closure path: Human config → operational acceptance checks/permissions, or review → goal rejection/approval → next run/delivery; no mission/identity conflict → legitimate ultimate-policy judgment → changed binding constitution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Human final approval does not automatically imply VSM S5; even a domain policy string is an execution constraint rather than an ultimate organizational identity dispute loop. - Evidence: domain/goal policy, review resolution, role authority. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Ultimate identity/purpose decisions may exist in the real operator&#39;s institution, outside this open-source single-user software boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1bce7f1a2ff3c599516a40edbd064de106784076/assessments/ag/agentwork.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1bce7f1a2ff3c599516a40edbd064de106784076\">1bce7f1 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T12:59:07+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Orchestragent/orchestragent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">orch</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Orchestragent/orchestragent/tree/533d06b440654e0f9c9654c80945a751796747e5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 533d06b440654e0f9c9654c80945a751796747e5\">533d06b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Generate and repair code in response to scoped user specification and feedback from tests and model calls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar source, incomplete interfaces, implementation errors, rejected model outputs and failing frozen tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a task solution/patch from specification, context and feedback, including changed implementation on failure. - Decision owner: Model-backed planner and builder calls in the installed run; deterministic code enforces schema, attempts and test acceptance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Worktrees, contextpack, prompt budgets, frozen tests, provider adaptors, retry ladder. - Closure path: Model plan → task/test contracts → model patch → sandbox test → accept or issue failure feedback → reattempt/replan/park. - Boundary reachability: plan and execute are first-party orchestrator operations reachable through supported CLI. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The planner and code model choose substantive task decomposition and edits; the scheduler does not author those solutions. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L715-L885, orchrun.py#L3247-L3477, orchrun.py#L3848-L3905. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for a reachable source-level loop; no provider-run success reproduced. - Caveats: The normal run requires configured model inference and an executable project environment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Distinct S1 units: Parallel independently model-backed task builders, each operating in its own task-specific worktree against the same shared integration branch. - Inter-S1 disturbance: A builder&#39;s code or contract can overlap another builder&#39;s changed files, produce a Git conflict, or invalidate a frozen test already passed by another task. - Attenuating coordination relation: Planned file ownership/dependencies, serialized merge lock, affected-tests integration gate and explicit capped rebasing of collided tasks. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Conflict or affected-test failure returns the named task to READY/rebase or retry with updated branch state rather than silently accepting both edits. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The implemented merge-collision-to-task-retry and cross-task test-interference gate regulate actual interaction failures between distinct coding units. - Function: Attenuate interference between parallel code-building units in a shared integration branch. - Disturbance / variety regulated: File ownership overlap, incompatible dependency interfaces, parallel Git conflicts and tests broken by another worker&#39;s merge. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Coordinate dependency/ownership and merge order, reject a conflicting commit and route the affected task back for a refreshed build. - Decision owner: Deterministic DAG scheduler, interface and file gates and integration merge lock; no separate autonomous coordination actor owns the arbitration policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Planned dependency edges, serialized integration, affected-test gate, rebase count and changed-worktree tests. - Closure path: Concurrent task outputs → merge collision/affected tests → reject/rebase/retry of specific task → next task execution on updated integration state. - Boundary reachability: execute, ready, merge are standard paths for concurrent task runs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The collision/feedback witness is function-specific and shipped, but the coordinator&#39;s decisive scheduling/merge arbitration is hard-coded; agent-owned S2 would need composition. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L2170-L2257, orchrun.py#L2420-L2475, orchrun.py#L3848-L3910. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Mere DAG edges would not establish S2 without the identified collision-response/return path.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Whole-system current view: The run&#39;s persisted task table, DONE/READY/RUNNING/PARKED statuses, worker occupancy, rate/spend counters and outstanding dependency graph. - Current-control decision scope: Adjust current concurrency and admissions, reorder ready work by dependency criticality, or stop a module with repeated parked tasks; all are currently code-controlled rather than model-owned decisions. - Function: Control the complete current spec-run portfolio&#39;s admissions, throughput, resource limits and stalled tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider rate-limit pressure, too many active model calls, spend caps, blocked dependencies and repeating module failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Change current concurrency, dispatch priority and task admissions or park a failed module based on observed whole-run state. - Decision owner: Deterministic scheduler (drain, room, throttle, breaker); models generate tasks but do not autonomously decide whole-system resource intervention. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AIMD limit, token/cache policies, dependency depth sorting, persistent state, failure counts. - Closure path: Ready/in-flight state and failure signals → scheduler resource/priority intervention → changed dispatch and retry conditions for multiple tasks. - Boundary reachability: Standard execution uses drain and applies current maxjobs/maxverifyjobs, rate and spend gates. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A whole-run feedback and resource-decision path exists, but its discretionary control policy is fixed in code, leaving agent ownership uncomposed. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L2170-L2294, orchrun.py#L2451-L2490. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium; no autonomous allocator or qualified human parent mode evidenced. - Caveats: Ordinary user-set quotas do not independently establish P parent governance.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: The planner&#39;s frozen test contract correctly expresses the original user requirements, notwithstanding a builder&#39;s failing implementation or allegation of wrong tests. - Ordinary reporting path: The builder&#39;s candidate code and sandbox failure result are reported to the retry ladder and orchestration state. - Complementary access path: A separately prompted Claude reviewer receives original specification, requirement mapping, frozen test sources and explicit dispute, not just the builder&#39;s success report. - Independence boundary: Separate persistent review ID, pinned input hash and validated testscorrect/testswrong verdict; the builder cannot directly rewrite frozen tests. - Who acts on findings: review closes a correct-test dispute or fixtests commits guarded test corrections and re-verifies affected candidate and prior merges. - Function: Independently challenge the frozen test contract against the original user specification when task failures indicate a test dispute. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Wrong or over-constraining tests drafted before implementation, misleading failure attribution and builder attempts to relax constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An exception-review model independently returns testscorrect or testswrong, with an explanation and guarded test edits; a wrong-test verdict can authorize corrective test revision. - Decision owner: Separate Claude review invocation using original specification, protected from the builder&#39;s own test-edit decision; deterministic code validates the verdict and safe application. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Immutable review inputs, durable review store, assertion-count no-weakening gate and regression check of already merged tasks. - Closure path: Failed worker/test dispute → independent review → validated finding → corrected frozen tests or rejection → saved candidate reverified / task retried. - Boundary reachability: review and fixtests are first-party retry-ladder operations; their execution is conditional, not an asserted always-on audit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer has the substantive adjudication right; the builder cannot directly alter the pre-frozen test contract, while code checks and transports the verdict. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L3010-L3140, orchreview.py. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for the dispute-triggered complementary path. - Caveats: Independence is functional/prompt-and-role separation, not a demonstrated separate provider organization; ordinary final pytest gates alone would not establish A.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - External distinction: Past external project specifications, sandbox/test outcomes and model-builder failures vary between runs; rule candidates are grounded in these encountered environmental distinctions, not only an internal heartbeat. - Future / prospective distinction: The A/B and replay checks compare candidate rules against distinct past specifications to select instructions expected to improve subsequent planning/building runs. - Adaptation option generated: Model-authored, cross-task reusable planner or builder lessons become candidate rules, tested with/without them and potentially activated at later checkpoints. - Path back into current capability / S3: Promoted rules enter the planner or builder prompt layer in a subsequent run, changing the model&#39;s future action repertoire, while harmful rules are retired. - Function: Learn prospective planning/building-rule changes from accumulated cross-run failures and outcomes, and return successful changes into future model prompts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Repeating planning defects, weak tests, recurring builder mistakes and the possibility that a proxy for eventual success becomes misleading. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model review/replanning/build attempts formulate generalizable lessons; A/B evidence evaluates whether a candidate helps, with checkpoint promotion and subsequent prompt influence. - Decision owner: Autonomous models propose the adaptive rule content from task observations; deterministic promotion statistics retain activation veto, so the agent does not own final promotion discretion in isolation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Rule store, paired trials and replay, proxy-vs-real Spearman trust test, lifecycle candidate → active → retired and prompt layer budget. - Closure path: Outcomes and model lessons from earlier runs → candidate rule → with/without evaluation → promote/retire at checkpoint → altered next-plan/builder instruction. - Boundary reachability: learn, evolve, settleplannerrules and builder rule trials are first-party modes (automatic rule learning disabled in frozen-policy modes). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Autonomous models originate strategic adaptation content, not merely execute a preselected fixed retry; promotion is a statistical safeguard rather than a model-authored option. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L1570-L1775, orchrun.py#L3888-L3925, orchrules.py#L1-L125. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium for prospectively reusable operating-policy learning; causal effectiveness not independently reproduced. - Caveats: Feedback comes chiefly from past run/task outcomes, not market sensing. If an evaluator requires a wholly independent outside-environment sensor rather than cross-run experience, S4 should be revisited; the observed loop is durable future capability change, not just current repair.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party whole-system identity or ultimate-policy dispute and legitimate-authority closure is evidenced for a spec-run organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe workspace edits, mismatched model routes, insufficient budgets and altered test contracts are addressed as operational guardrails. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Bind an ultimate policy/identity judgment to later operations; no such accountable agent or legitimate parent governance loop is shipped. - Decision owner: User chooses configuration and optional frozen policy; model reviews resolve tests and task strategy, not organizational identity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Policy store, sandbox boundary, explicit model settings, pinned planning inputs, cancel/park limits and review gate. - Closure path: Configuration and safety gates constrain a run; no S5-level issue/appeal → authoritative decision → returned binding identity policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Guarded model choices and an immutable execution policy are not themselves an S5 constitutional decision. - Evidence: orchpolicy.py, orchrun.py#L715-L815. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Maintainer policy and a human&#39;s optional local run selection are outside this selected recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/987160f64eccd59b120a491c9f00becefacad4b3/assessments/or/orchestragent-orch.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 987160f64eccd59b120a491c9f00becefacad4b3\">987160f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T14:25:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mimshins/ork\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ork</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mimshins/ork/tree/e97d75c554095ff41d79ef91fafbbdc8bcfc8b64\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e97d75c554095ff41d79ef91fafbbdc8bcfc8b64\">e97d75c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Claude sessions in a workflow transform requests into code, specifications, reviews and other local project outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user requests, repository state, test failures, tool results and child reports within each session&#39;s bounded task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose concrete implementation/review actions and revise work from the tool, branch and report feedback. - Decision owner: model-driven Claude agent node in the first-party runtime; an operator supplies the initial task and permissions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: graph activation, SDK/CLI driver, tool allowlists, worktrees, event store, session budget and schema validation. - Closure path: agent chooses tools and edits, receives tool/result feedback, changes work and returns a structured report to its graph/leader. - Boundary reachability: claude.worker/claude.leader nodes and their tool/report bridge are supported installed node types, exercised structurally by shipped example workflows; no third-party agent&#39;s hidden control architecture is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: actions in a live session remain discretionary even though workflow graph edges, budgets and output transport are deterministic. - Evidence: scheduler built-ins and delegation, delegation port, factory example. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider execution was not performed in this review; operator credentials and the chosen workflow are prerequisites.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: regulate competing write access among parallel S1 sessions in the same run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two concurrent writing agents can attempt overlapping files/folders or shared named resources, introducing overwrites or merge conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: agents select resources to claim, release them and respond to a refused claim by choosing another task or negotiating with the holder; the registry deterministically detects overlap. - Decision owner: agent session for claim selection and the corrective work choice; deterministic claim registry for overlap verdict, holder identity, event delivery and automatic release. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: advisory claim registry keyed by normalized paths, refusal and release notices, worktree isolation and message channels. - Closure path: a conflicting claim is refused with holder identity; the agent receives that response, can communicate/replan, and may retry after a release notification, changing the subsequent S1 work target. - Boundary reachability: claim/release tools are supplied to writing Claude nodes when a run exposes parallel writers; the scheduler binds the run-scoped claim port. This is a shipped operating mode, not a hypothetical custom integration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Ork does not choose the agent&#39;s alternative work in response to contention; its registry only determines the fixed overlap rule. Claim discipline is advisory, so not every actual edit is protected. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently running writer leader/worker nodes or spawned worker copies with separate tasks and checkouts. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping resource/path edits and resultant destructive interference, specifically documented as the reason for claims. - Attenuating coordination relation: explicit per-resource claim refusal, release, holder/contender notification and optional peer messaging. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the refused agent sees the holding node and can work elsewhere or ask it; release notice tells the agent to claim again before editing. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: resource overlap is checked in executable first-party code, with a conflict-specific refusal/release feedback loop; message passing is only a support channel. - Evidence: claim registry implementation, Claude claim tool, claims and message specification. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: autonomous conflict handling is agent-instructed but not hard-enforced and was not exercised with a provider here; if the claimed function is defined solely as lock acquisition, that deterministic subdecision is not agent-owned.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: control a multi-child run&#39;s current commitments and exceptions at a configured leader/whole-run boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: child failure, run budget/concurrency saturation, exhausted revision loops, and conflicting current delivery commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the leader can choose whether and where to delegate/retry in light of child reports; an owner can answer an exhausted-loop exception, while configuration author sets the initial hard limits. - Decision owner: first-party leader/owner decision ports are available; workflow author must compose a genuinely whole-run supervisory objective and authority envelope. The deterministic runner owns enforcement, not the resource-policy judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: slot/budget accounting, maxWorkers cap, statuses, reports, loop counter, human fallback and halt semantics. - Closure path: delegated children return outcomes and costs; the leader can select follow-on work, or answer an exhausted loop to stop/extend/go onward; the scheduler executes the selected transition. - Boundary reachability: leader tools, child reports and owner-question/answer loop are shipped in the scheduler and Claude runtime; their S3-specific whole-run composition depends on the declared workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: these are real current-intervention primitives, but a generic leader&#39;s task division alone does not establish a default autonomous whole-run S3 owner for arbitrary user workflows. This supports the narrower constructor C, not universal A. - Whole-system current view: available child reports, run status, costs and configured limits; whether they form an actionable whole-system view is workflow-dependent. - Current-control decision scope: child commitments and retry/loop continuation under configured budgets; no assertion that the agent can revise operator-specified global policy. - Evidence: leader/child delegation, loop owner decision, scheduler budget. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: no parent-mode suffix from ordinary human gates, pause/stop or local approval; no runtime end-to-end current-control trace tested.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: inspect implementation claims through separately tasked agents that can directly read the produced branch rather than rely only on the builder&#39;s report. - Disturbance / variety regulated: defects in the builder&#39;s asserted completion, including security, correctness, UX and code-quality issues overlooked by ordinary production. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independent readonly reviewer sessions judge the produced work and return pass/changesrequested; the builder then receives review status and revises in a bounded loop. - Decision owner: separate reviewer Claude agents in the first-party software-factory opt-in mode; graph routing enforces returned verdicts, but is not the audit judge. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: readonly checkout, fresh reviewer sessions, join statuses, bounded edge maxRounds, agent approver and optional human publication gate. - Closure path: builder branch is inspected by separately running security/UX/code reviewers; a changesrequested result routes back to builder and triggers repair; passing reviews route to the approver. - Boundary reachability: examples/software-factory.ork.yaml is a distributed executable Ork workflow invoking the installed reviewer node types with readonly project access. The path is an opt-in mode, not assumed present in every workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer models decide the audit verdict based on direct artifact access; deterministic joins merely transport outcomes. Their separation is context/process isolation, not institutional independence from the operator or model supplier. - Claim being audited: builder&#39;s assertion that a branch implements the spec correctly and safely. - Ordinary reporting path: builder&#39;s status, summary and artifact/branch report to the workflow. - Complementary access path: security/UX/code agents in readonly workspaces inspect the actual branch and surrounding code, not just the builder&#39;s summary. - Independence boundary: different agent sessions, reviewer roles and readonly source access; all are authored within one user-defined workflow, so a malicious author can weaken the separation. - Who acts on findings: builder corrects the branch when any review requests changes; approver examines the passing result before the user-controlled publication step. - Evidence: factory workflow, PR-review workflow, worktree/delegation behavior. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: source-grounded installed example path, not independently executed S3 evaluation or a universal independent-audit service; routine sequential QA in an arbitrary workflow would not automatically qualify.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party externally oriented prospective adaptation loop is established at the assessed run boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing external demands or future capabilities could require adaptation, but no shipped owner closes that function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an Ork-run S4. - Decision owner: none demonstrated; workflow authors or maintainers may revise YAML and Ork code outside the run. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: captured feedback, event store, local feedback list/note/dismiss, context compiler and user-authored scripts. - Closure path: no shipped feedback-to-accepted-change/lesson path into future current run capability at this revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: collection and grouping of past errors are not themselves a future-oriented model/options/return loop. - Evidence: feedback spec with explicit partial implementation, feedback guide, roadmap. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a user may build a separate adaptation workflow; that is not shipped S4 closure in the declared default system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy adjudication path for a workflow organization is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: identity or policy tensions can arise, but Ork&#39;s gates and constraints concern routine task/runtime permissions and delivery. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped S5-specific issue, legitimate authority judgment and return-to-operation path. - Decision owner: workflow author/operator defines configuration and controls ordinary approval; those actions are not an evidenced identity-policy process. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: YAML settings, tool allowlists, budget caps, askuser, human gates, CLI run controls and local auth. - Closure path: ordinary approvals/stop and permission decisions affect run progression, not an established ultimate-policy matter. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a user or agent can edit instruction text or choose publication, but no system-specific S5 right is constructed or closed. - Evidence: workflow format, message and decision controls, factory human gate. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the external human/project may have its own legitimate S5; Ork&#39;s default standard distribution does not supply that governance loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/06adb32ca65514fbe9a0f7c625cdfe64b2c702de/assessments/or/ork.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 06adb32ca65514fbe9a0f7c625cdfe64b2c702de\">06adb32 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T17:43:47+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DarkDvr/Synapse\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Synapse</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DarkDvr/Synapse/tree/b91345750a7d8ec1fca1557cdf33613762794223\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b91345750a7d8ec1fca1557cdf33613762794223\">b913457 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomous local production and checking of a specific delegated project deliverable. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ambiguous requirements, codebase/tool errors, rendered defects and incomplete subtasks encountered during task execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Role model chooses its own next implementation/test tool after observing results. - Decision owner: Role-specific model agent, reached through Synapse-created engine session; human owns separate scope/permission gates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OpenCode adapter, per-role tool rules, session brief, tool transcript, provider retries, bounded worker runner and written-artifact ledger. - Closure path: Model tool call→real engine execution→tool feedback→continuing role turn→worker report/files, then return to Architect. - Boundary reachability: Shipped Multi mode makes genuine model-backed role sessions rather than invoking a development mock. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The role&#39;s model has task-sensitive tool/repair choice; Synapse supplies the enclosing execution and evidence channel. - Evidence: OpenCode adapter, runs.js, plans.js. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high on reachable code path; no independent Windows-provider execution. - Caveats: Actual model quality, installed/packaged engine availability and end-to-end completion were not live-tested.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Feedback-based regulation of interference or oscillation among operational S1 task agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent edits, shared project plan corruption and worker cross-role overwrite risks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No specific agent-owned inter-worker collision detection→mediation→returned adjustment evidenced. - Decision owner: Deterministic harness serialization and folder rules; Architect decides task sequence as S3 commitment, not evidenced as S2 conflict mediation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: One-active-worker busy guard per project, roleWriteRules, plan ownership, role folders and assignment ladder. - Closure path: Serial admission and static write rules prevent some conflicts but do not inspect actual inter-unit interference and negotiate mutual change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Transport, isolation and top-down task allocation alone are insufficient for S2 at this recursion. - Evidence: runs.js#L546, plans.js. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for the reviewed first-party orchestration paths. - Caveats: A model could discuss competing tasks in an individual session, but no reusable S2-specific interference closure was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: Architect reads the project-wide plan.json task statuses, blocked items, confirmed features and per-worker completion reports grounded in runs.jsonl and actual file/tool records. - Current-control decision scope: Architect alone accepts/rejects task completion, alters the whole plan and selects corrective work and next role commitments within approved project scope; a runner&#39;s retry/backoff merely enforces constraints. - Function: Current project-wide commitment, progress and corrective task regulation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Partial deliverables, faulty worker claims, blocked tasks, failed providers, scope drift and new defects threatening the approved project goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge each reported worker outcome, update the plan, mark done/retry/blocked, reprioritize or delegate the next current task. - Decision owner: Model-driven Architect in Multi mode, constrained by operator-approved features and optional automatic continuation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan.json as Architect-only authority, actual runs.jsonl, injected post-worker report, task-role assignment, retry fallback, progress/status rail. - Closure path: Worker run evidence→synthetic report→Architect evaluation/update of plan→next delegate or required operator escalation; Keep building enables continuation, while off returns after each judged task. - Boundary reachability: delegate MCP, run ledger, handleTurn, report and wake are connected in shipped Multi mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the Architect leaves recorded runs and deterministic stops but not discretionary whole-project acceptance, replanning or corrective commitment. - Evidence: runs.js#L2741, plans.js#L1270, delegate MCP. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high; project-control closure is code-visible, not a live success claim. - Caveats: An operator can pause or steer a task, but the documented toggles do not independently establish a distinct parent-governed S3 decision mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: Implementing worker&#39;s claim that the shipped build is usable, faithful to approved design and free of target regressions. - Ordinary reporting path: Coder sends its own transcript, modified-file list and completion statement to Architect through runs.jsonl and the synthetic result message. - Complementary access path: Separate QC model role uses the delivered application, renders/screenshots UI, diffs approved mock, tests unusual inputs and writes reproducible findings with IDs independently of worker prose. - Independence boundary: QC has its own configurable model/session and role folder; it cannot edit the build and coders cannot write the QC findings folder under Synapse&#39;s first-party role rules. This is role separation, not tamper-proof institutional independence. - Who acts on findings: Architect reads QC output and delegates the numbered fix to a coder, then assigns verification of that exact repro to QC again, up to the three-round cap. - Function: Independent role-level challenge to implementation claims using real build behavior and a returned quality verdict. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Worker reports that conceal unusable UI, clipping, misleading states, poor validation or regressions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: QC chooses and executes adversarial/use-oriented checks, records reproducible findings and re-verifies fixes separately from implementing coders. - Decision owner: Dedicated configured qc model/session, distinct from coder role, with its own findings area and read-only stance toward the build. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: QC mandate to render/use the built artifact, screenshot/mock diffs, finding IDs and repros, separate folder permissions, bounded three-round QC/fix/verification plan. - Closure path: QC direct inspection→qcfindings/qc-N.md with evidence→Architect receives report and assigns specific fix task→QC re-runs repros and verifies correction. - Boundary reachability: First-party QC role, delegated real engine session, report loop and role-write restrictions are shipped in Multi mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: QC independently determines and reports actual product defects; the Architect, not QC, retains current-control commitment changes. - Evidence: plans.js#L700, plans.js#L1342, runs.js#L1002. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium: independently tasked source-visible QC path, with no performed cross-provider execution or efficacy audit. - Caveats: The product does not establish strong organizational or cryptographic isolation; different role/model configuration and artifact-based repro constitute the limited claimed independence. If QC is never delegated in a particular plan, the optional path is inactive.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Sensing external/future needs and developing options returned to current project capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unanticipated user needs and future features for the product under construction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An Inventor model proposes and ranks extra features; operator chooses scope and Architect links confirmed features to tasks. Whether this amounts to an external sensing→adaptation→S3 loop is unresolved. - Decision owner: Inventor generates options and operator accepts features; no proved future-environment evidence trail connecting the two. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Inventor role mandate, optional local search tools, ideas file, confirmed/rejected features.json and Architect planning instructions. - Closure path: Proposed feature→human selection→confirmed feature/task is explicit; source does not establish that the proposal derived from an observed external prospective distinction rather than imagined additions to a current wish. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Generating project feature ideas is not, alone, a demonstrated VSM S4 process; the active inventor path prevents a defensible categorical absence claim. - Evidence: plans.js#L660, plans.js#L1287, README. - Basis: structural - Confidence: bounded uncertainty. - Caveats: Direct evidence of actual external future-condition observation and its governing choice would resolve the state; neither was reproduced at this source pin.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Legitimate ultimate-policy or system identity dispute resolution at project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicts over project identity, ultimate purpose and governing policy rather than current task scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party identity-level issue→authority decision→returned governing policy path shown. - Decision owner: Human operator chooses product features, permission and continuity toggles; those remain operational/requirement decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Feature selection forms, prompt constraints, role-specific permission rules and operator approval. - Closure path: Scope selection reaches project plan, but no recognized ultimate-policy decision channel governs a dispute about the system&#39;s identity. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Choosing which mock to build or whether to continue is not an S5 legitimacy mechanism. - Evidence: plans.js#L1287, automation.js, permissions.js. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: External real-world product governance or developer organizational decisions are out of the assessed standard-distribution boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7211b7addb042e94e57c4d3d7934a31e2d314368/assessments/da/darkdvr-synapse.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7211b7addb042e94e57c4d3d7934a31e2d314368\">7211b7a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T17:22:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HG-ha/Argusai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ArgusAI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HG-ha/Argusai/tree/5f7aa0844ae0341f3563da2a4d171e3fb797d3ac\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5f7aa0844ae0341f3563da2a4d171e3fb797d3ac\">5f7aa08 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out security-analysis and general assistant operations through model-selected tools and separate task-performing children. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain code/traffic observations, new user materials, scan errors, tool outputs, environment and evidence changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select and sequence domain tools, analyze returned evidence and choose next investigation/validation/report action. - Decision owner: Configured native model-bearing ArgusAI assistant, or connected model-bearing child where given an explicit bounded role; external ACP engines are not credited as ArgusAI&#39;s independent cognitive design. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model/tool call loop, domain tool catalog, run/stream state, content archives, confirmation broker and scoped child capability projection. - Closure path: User-defined scope → model/tool call on live workspace → task/evidence output → model inspects and continues/terminates → report or next task state. - Boundary reachability: The native model/tool engine and first-party domain registry are wired into normal GUI/CLI operation, and child tool selection is registered in the shipped assistant. An external ACP model is an alternative supported operating mode, not required for the native witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses substantive task actions; deterministic policy, progress guards and run queues enforce limits rather than owning the choice. - Evidence: assistant loop, tool methods, child execution, README runtime. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for model/tool-path existence; provider execution was not replayed here. - Caveats: A standalone scan engine or GUI table does not own S1 without the configured model session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: Independently executing persistent Team Room members, each with distinct objective, tool projection, history, inbox and ongoing work; temporary subagents also exist but do not communicate directly. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Repeated peer status chatter loops, duplicate or out-of-order targeted requests, interruption of busy specialists, concurrent competition for shared member capacity and missed handoff dependencies. - Attenuating coordination relation: Team member system context requires model discretion to respond only when its role/objective is implicated, to make concrete @role handoffs and otherwise call teamroompass. Durable inboxes deduplicate input references, queue while busy and preserve explicit request ordering; capacity waiters resume as slots release. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A specialist reviews actual targeted peer/leader messages after finishing its current work, chooses to pass, answer with new evidence or issue an exact-role request; that message enters peer inboxes and changes their next work/action choice. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The explicit model-facing anti-loop rule and selective peer-response/handoff control mitigate inter-agent chatter/attention oscillation, while delivery sequencing/dedup solves concrete concurrent-message interference. A generic chat log alone would not establish the agent-owned decision. - Function: Maintain compatible peer coordination without interrupt/reply storms across simultaneous team work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Redundant chat cycles, missed role-specific requests, reentrant live work and shared member-slot interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide when own role must answer an inter-agent request, pass unproductive chatter, or send a bounded dependency/handoff to another specialist. - Decision owner: Model-bearing persistent member agents exercising model-facing Team Room rules; deterministic queue/dedup/capacity checks only supply delivery discipline. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Separate Team Room log/cursors and pending-input queue, selective @role message route, teamroompass, input reference dedup and member concurrency limits. - Closure path: Peer message/redundant chatter or directed dependency → model member evaluates role fit → pass or targeted correction/handoff → queue records/limits the message → affected member consumes it and adjusts work. - Boundary reachability: Persistent Team Rooms, first-party member prompt and member tools are shipped in the standard GUI/assistant mode; they are not optional third-party skills. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The decision whether to escalate/pass/answer is model discretion. Serial/held input alone would not support A, so the positive is restricted to the standing-team model-facing selective coordination path. - Evidence: agent role prompt and member queue, Team Room routing and read cursors, architecture/queue contract. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; no empirical measured chat-loop reduction is claimed. - Caveats: Temporary spawnteam has no peer messaging; this S2 A is confined to the separately supported persistent-team mode, not all single-agent sessions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Whole-system current view: Parent assistant can read the complete Todo tree, current task/job progress, team roster, child notices, evidence links and tool outputs; owner GUI sees team state and can edit/steer running membership and assignments. - Current-control decision scope: Model chooses investigation phases, work assignments, task continuation, relevant members, tool batches and response to child results. Owner separately changes standing-team staffing, objective/role assignments and active scope of current work, with next-turn effect. - Function: Coordinate immediate work commitments and evidence production for a whole user objective. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Specialist readiness, task phase dependencies, uncertain evidence and external tool errors, changing user direction, busy child agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model creates/updates whole-goal Todo plan, delegates and redirects workers; parent owner can edit or pause team members, change scope and direct next assigned work. - Decision owner: Main configured model for autonomous management; installation owner via Team GUI/member update for the parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Todo plan, run locks, live UI, child registry, Team Room and group message stores, confirmation/permission gates. - Closure path: Whole-goal evidence and child notices → model changes priorities/delegation → different agent/tool actions; owner observes team, edits member/assignments or sends new instruction → persisted metadata and injected typed event change subsequent child operation. - Boundary reachability: Both assistant model tools (settodos, spawnteam, agent input) and owner GUI team/member edit controls are first-party installed entry points; they return into running agent sessions rather than isolated dashboards. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Autonomous model retains current resource choices; the user separately holds a legitimate parent current-control role over the same local team. Hard permissions/queues alone do not count as a second owner. - Evidence: model planning/delegation methods, child control/notifications, owner team/member editing. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high within installed modes; no actual provider-backed orchestration run was executed. - Caveats: User task interjections are not credited alone; the distinct parent mode is the current-control owner GUI plus persistent member state returned to operation.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: Distinct independent examination of operational claims via complementary raw reality rather than ordinary task reporting; full closure cannot be established confidently from the reviewed default distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Misleading child report, inaccurate scan/finding, incomplete task claim. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently challenge a worker/result with raw Case/Artifact/Tool evidence and return a corrective finding to control; whether a governed non-producer audit owner routinely exercises it is unresolved. - Decision owner: Main assistant may inspect material and can instantiate readonly specialists; the built-in reporter role consolidates findings but is not shown to have a mandatory independently sourced veto/repair channel. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Case/Evidence original references, readonly subagents, result summaries, operator review, tool histories and durable event logs. - Closure path: Child or domain output → main assistant can reread raw evidence and assign further validation → correction to tasks is possible, but a shipped independent complementary inspection owner and repeatable corrective return are not fully proven. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Source supports model review as ordinary operations and a purpose-composable review role, yet cannot distinguish a true independent S3 audit from a task-specific secondary analysis in all supported modes. - Evidence: evidence/case store, main review path, readonly specialist presets/child roles. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium for real audit capability, low for verified S3-specific ownership. - Caveats: webaudit reporter summarizes other roles and the human inspects findings; neither automatically supplies independent unfiltered ground-truth inspection. No presumed external security review is credited.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Adapt future patrol priorities, scheduled investigation or long-lived operational capability from external change; specific closed adaptation ownership remains unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changed attack surface, new file/log events, future alerts and cross-session findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose and enact a prospective change to future monitoring/adaptation, distinct from simply completing current steps or running fixed Cron commands. - Decision owner: A model may call first-party cronupsert and alter future work triggers; an installation owner can also configure schedules. However no clearly evidenced shipped model process compares new environment trends, decides a durable revised future capability, and closes that decision back into later operating configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cron tasks with interval/cron/webhook/file-change triggers, persisted jobs/run records, long-term session memory and task/Case references. - Closure path: External event → scheduled session inspects current artifacts and may update Todos/schedule → later trigger executes; the decisive model&#39;s prospective adaptation criterion and actual feedback-based reconfiguration are not resolved at this pin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model-selectable scheduler plus memory offers a credible first-party adaptation candidate, but scheduling fixed repetitions and reusing prior facts alone are not an established S4 owner. - Evidence: model-visible cronupsert catalog, persistent scheduler, scheduler GUI routes, long-term memory. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium for future triggers, insufficient for whether they constitute positive S4 adaptation. - Caveats: ? is caused by an identified plausible shipped path, not by a hypothetical future feature; fixed scheduling should not be mistaken for itself owning outside-and-then intelligence.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: What a persistent local Team is for, which named specialist roles constitute it, and which role objectives/tool-power boundaries bind members across sessions. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: The legitimate self-hosted installation/team owner can create, recharter and edit its standing team and member objectives through first-party GUI/API; no agent-owned constitutional authority to replace that owner&#39;s ultimate charter is established here. - Return-to-operation path: Owner changes standing team purpose or member role/objective/capabilities → persisted team/member record and pending typed metadata update → next member turn receives new role/objective; tool capability projection/confirmation mode enforces limits. - Function: Maintain binding purpose and role identity of the persistent team as a supported parent-governed arrangement. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changed organizational assignment, durable role constitution and owner-level policy over specialist identity and allowed operating repertoire. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Authoritatively change a team/member&#39;s stated continuing purpose, role identity and authorization scope for subsequent work, beyond changing a single tool call. - Decision owner: Installation owner in the supported human-operated GUI/team setup mode; model tools can request team changes but no distinct self-authorized autonomous ultimate-policy closure is claimed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persisted Team metadata, member records, edited role/objective, capability projection and queued metadataupdatepending typed event. - Closure path: Parent decides a standing team/role-purpose revision → updateteammember persists the charter and emits event → member&#39;s future context/inbox incorporates it → subsequent agent work follows updated purpose/policy. - Boundary reachability: Creating and updating persistent teams and members are shipped, first-party GUI/API features, not repository maintainer policy or an external integration. The member worker reads revised metadata on its next safe turn. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The credited S5 mode is owner-authored standing team constitution, not the assistant approving its own tools or choosing current task steps; S3-level task steering remains separately recorded. - Evidence: persistent team create/update, member metadata revision and next-turn notice, member role/permission API schema. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium for the local standing team identity loop. - Caveats: One-off job objectives, approval toggles and permission flags alone are not S5. This claim is limited to the persistent-team role/purpose constitution, not governance of unrelated ArgusAI installations or external security organizations.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/49cc3492e73efe3cbdcbc2bc63dac903deaa2cfd/assessments/ar/argusai.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 49cc3492e73efe3cbdcbc2bc63dac903deaa2cfd\">49cc349 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T22:45:47+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/andreseloysv/Tirith\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tirith</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/andreseloysv/Tirith/tree/be358146324a8a9c98e6649938205328160c79cf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision be358146324a8a9c98e6649938205328160c79cf\">be35814 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-operated coding and repository task execution in separately launched worktree lanes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown code changes, failing tools/tests, context limits and incomplete user-specified plan items. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select edits, terminal/repository operations and subsequent task actions based on observed tool results and brief constraints. - Decision owner: Configured coding-agent model operating each spawned lane; manager is another model-mediated operational conversation for on-call tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model CLI adapters, lane brief, isolated worktree, agent log reader, plan report, hooks, tool gates, retry and timeout bounds. - Closure path: Open plan item → manager/launcher starts coding-model CLI → model acts in worktree and observes results → reports done/partial/blocked → reviewer/manager feedback may cause a corrected new turn. - Boundary reachability: The shipping lane run/launch command builds supported first-party agent aliases and reads real provider output; plan gate and dispatcher route results into the active project plan. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Plan reservation, test gates and Git merge logic cannot choose the substantive code edit; only the model agent does. - Evidence: agent runner, launcher, launch contract. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for wired agent invocation and returned status. - Caveats: Agent binaries/provider accounts are external prerequisites; this review did not execute a paid live coding session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent duplicate concurrent ownership and overlapping write scopes between independent operational lanes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two plan workers claim the same item, write the same folder or operate on an incomplete prerequisite, causing stale or conflicting source changes. - Distinct S1 units: Multiple separately launched model-agent lanes in individual worktrees, possibly concurrent on separate tasks. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Same task ID can be claimed twice, and explicit write paths can overlap including ancestor/descendant folders. - Attenuating coordination relation: SQLite claim/attempt-token transaction, dependency check, lease/heartbeat, write-path overlap check and reported claim refusal; centralized merge queue serializes integration separately. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Conflicting claims block launch, a missing predecessor remains blocked until actually merged, and released/failed reservations become visible to new launcher/manager decisions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Path-specific atomic exclusivity and stale-attempt fencing address an identified inter-agent resource collision; failure and recovery change which subsequent S1 unit may begin work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Determine conflict admission and reclaim/release an expired claim after checking worker liveness. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic coordination constructor (SQLite rules and collector recovery), not a model that can change the reservation policy in the operating mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: coordination.sqlite3, permanent item IDs, explicit declared writes, lease and attempt token, heartbeat, worker inbox with idempotent messages. - Closure path: Candidate lane → transaction checks existing owners, prerequisites and write overlap → accepted lease or refused conflict → manager/launcher receives available work or blockage and revises later starts. - Boundary reachability: Standard launch acquires reservation before creating worktree/spawning agent; hook/report paths check attempt token; collector recovers dead-owner claims. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Models select task priorities and write declarations but the final conflict verdict, arbitration and lease recovery run deterministically. - Evidence: coordination implementation, coordination contract, merge queue. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for reservation conflicts in declared scopes. - Caveats: Legacy items without declared write paths have no additional path reservation; independent raw shell writes remain bounded by other tool/worktree gates, not this S2 path.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate the current cross-project plan/worktree portfolio, agent assignments, blocked work, integration and escalation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open work without a worker, stalled/rejected reviews, failed stages, WIP congestion, exhausted agent credits and owner-inbox backlog. - Whole-system current view: Manager brief/queue and Tirith commands expose open plan items, active lanes, status, review/merge queue and recent reports; operator dashboard shows all projects, agents, pipeline results and current owner actions. - Current-control decision scope: Manager chooses split, launch, combine, wait, retry, drop, or ask operator about a current item and handles failed reviews; operator can manually plan-edit, launch/stop agents, switch autopilot and resolve pending current decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Base model manager selects current operational commitments across plan items; human parent can directly alter commitments and stop/relaunch under the installed owner UI. - Decision owner: Base A — persistent on-call manager model. Parent P — human owner acting through Tirith desktop/inbox/plan UI; collector enforces allowed decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Resumable manager session, supervisor queue, autopilot proposals, plan gate, lane launcher, merge WIP limits and auto-repair, user inbox and owner-only actions. - Closure path: Base: collector finds ready/failed work → manager reads its queue/plan → decides agent assignment or follow-up → launched lanes and later results change board/plan and manager&#39;s next turn. Parent: owner sees status/alerts → changes plan, runs/stops lane or toggles autopilot → dispatcher/manager adopt changed current work state. - Boundary reachability: Managerd and dispatch are wired into every collector pass; owner inbox, Tauri commands and plan edits are first-party, directly updating the same live plan/dispatch state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model decides which issue to start, combine or defer based on the live portfolio; deterministic plan/merge gates and load limits enforce but do not choose those priorities. Human current-control is an independent supported mode. - Evidence: manager rules/session, autopilot handoff, inbox contract, mergeq. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for shipped control paths. - Caveats: A manager decision may fail or wait despite the tools. Optional CEO audit influences management but does not replace the base task manager with a guaranteed autonomous global governor.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently check the manager&#39;s account of progress/efficiency against raw collector operational measurements and send corrective directives. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A manager can inaccurately claim progress while no lanes run, reviews are stuck, costs grow or main CI remains red; ordinary manager conversation does not expose all such evidence. - Claim being audited: Whether the manager reliably turns the plan into verified mainline work within the operator&#39;s targets. - Ordinary reporting path: Manager&#39;s own chat, inbox answers, plan/queue decisions and per-lane status reports. - Complementary access path: An opt-in separate fresh CEO model lane gets collector-computed merged/started/review-health counts, scoreboards, age of pending requests and operational CI data; it does not read manager&#39;s private memory/full transcript or accept reports as ground truth. - Independence boundary: CEO has a fresh separate model session, read-only source environment and bounded raw-data snapshot independent of the manager&#39;s claimed status; both run under the same host/provider configuration and the operator controls targets. - Who acts on findings: CEO model judges findings and sends checkable directives to the manager inbox; for budget/code/target proposals it asks the human, who decides. Next CEO runs recheck whether directives improved the measured numbers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Make its own evidence-based operational audit judgment, issue corrective policy-level manager directives and test subsequent efficacy from independent raw counters. - Decision owner: Separate model-driven CEO reviewer in an enabled first-party mode; deterministic claimcheck and stages remain support for worker validation rather than this audit owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Optional CEO schedule/force-run entry point, fresh read-only lane, collector measurement snapshot, untrusted-data delimiters, typed result validation, findings/directives records and manager inbox. - Closure path: Measured operational discrepancy → CEO judgment from fresh snapshot → model directive/proposal delivered → manager changes current handling or owner resolves proposal → next CEO pass checks effects and reopens/escalates findings. - Boundary reachability: dispatch.run calls ceo.run on every cycle with [ceo] enabled; ceo.start launches a configured model review lane, and ceo.deliver writes actual manager directives/owner proposals. This opt-in path is shipped, not the planned watchdog. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Snapshot and typed parser do not decide which finding matters or which directive to issue; the distinct CEO model owns that judgment, while worker claimcheck and ordinary tests are deterministic checks. - Evidence: CEO implementation, dispatcher wiring, CEO contract, ordinary claim checker. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for first-party independently wired audit mode; no live CEO model output was reproduced. - Caveats: CEO is off by default. Its code-based snapshot does not prove factual claims outside its observed metrics; some planned CEO watchdog/ratings functions are not shipped.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party loop develops environmental/future options and returns a strategic capability adaptation into active portfolio operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model or tooling changes, future project opportunities and emerging external constraints might change strategy, but regular manager and CEO mostly inspect current/past project performance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Compare prospective external options, select a change to organizational capability and return it to current S3; no such complete chain is established. - Decision owner: Operator/developer chooses provider/model/target configuration; manager decides present plan items and CEO inspects operational KPIs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model fallback chain, Meta summaries, research-capable lanes, plan refresh, CEO targets and autopilot proposal queue. - Closure path: CEO may propose an internal code or budget change and owner may create a task, but there is no required external-environment future-option model or adaptive strategic S4-to-S3 control loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Internal throughput monitoring, recovery, scheduling and task planning are not outside-and-then intelligence even if a manager/CEO calls them policy. - Evidence: manager decisions, CEO targets, autopilot. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the bounded absent future function. - Caveats: A user can assign prospective market research work, but that is still a requested S1 task without evidenced strategic S4 return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No separately closed first-party constitutional mission/ultimate-policy dilemma resolution at the project portfolio recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Human-only credentials/payment choices, agent tool safety, target budgets, review and merge policy; these are operational risk/priority bounds. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Resolve a conflict about enduring mission/ethos or ultimate authority between current operations and adaptation, and govern later operations through the decision. - Decision owner: Human owner handles personal/financial decisions and sets target values; CEO can recommend targets and manager execution policy, but not replace owner&#39;s ultimate authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Owner gate/inbox, CEO proposals/approve-decline record, fixed plan/tool/merge hooks, config.toml and planned rules. - Closure path: A CEO recommendation can become an owner approval and plan item for code/budget/targets; this closes scoped operational improvement/authorization, not a demonstrated identity-policy controversy and constitutional return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Hard rules and a human-final decision are not automatically S5; the cited proposal classes are about task, spend and productivity rather than ultimate identity of the system. - Evidence: owner gate, CEO proposal/decision lifecycle, inbox contract. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; this is bounded absence, not denial of owner&#39;s real-world governance power. - Caveats: External maintainers and project owners may adopt lasting identity policies beyond the shipped operating workflow.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/75421e2aa581887f73a20a8dc5dc598fe23e4738/assessments/ti/tirith.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 75421e2aa581887f73a20a8dc5dc598fe23e4738\">75421e2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T19:59:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yusa-imit/colony\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">colony</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yusa-imit/colony/tree/b1a7679ac299bf4be2e278cfb25616fac52b3365\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b1a7679ac299bf4be2e278cfb25616fac52b3365\">b1a7679 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Adaptively produce software changes and work artifacts for a claimed task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unspecified implementation steps, code reality, failing checks, rebases and local task obstacles. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose implementation, tool sequence, code fixes, testing and completion or release of the current claimed task. - Decision owner: Individual model-bearing worker operating inside Colony&#39;s task protocol. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claude Code CLI process, isolated clone, turn prompt, MCP tools, tests, Git hub, budget gate. - Closure path: Current task/goal → worker reads code → chooses changes → checks observed result → repairs or pushes verified work → records task completion. - Boundary reachability: Colony launches real workers with first-party prompt and task tools on its standard command path; model/service availability is external. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model workers choose real next actions and interpret test feedback; the deterministic board does not write code or select solutions. - Evidence: prompts.rs#L38-L80, worker.rs, hub.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for operating ownership; no external provider-backed execution in this review. - Caveats: The coding cognition is supplied by an installed external Claude Code worker; Colony owns the protocol/orchestration that directs it, not the model internals.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: Separate code-writing Claude Code worker processes, each owning a different claimed task and isolated clone. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Competing task claims, duplicate work, conflicting changes to shared files and unmet task dependencies. - Attenuating coordination relation: Workers observe atomic claim feedback and peer messages, change their commitments, reserve dependency-blocked work, negotiate overlap and integrate only after a non-force Git rebase. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A refused claim makes the worker choose another item; a peer mention or rejected push leads to coordination, rebase and retesting before completing its own task. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The first-party protocol connects a named inter-worker collision to its observed refusal or conflict, then instructs the model worker to change its plan and return a non-conflicting commitment. The mechanical lock alone would not suffice. - Function: Attenuate interference between parallel workers changing the same goal/repository and return conflict signals to those workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing claims, duplicate task proposals, overlapping edits, unmet dependencies and stale peer assumptions can destabilize otherwise productive S1 workers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Re-select work after a rejected claim, renegotiate task boundaries via peer chat, revise after conflicting Git changes, or reserve/release an interdependent task. - Decision owner: Peer model workers interpret the conflicting board/chat/Git feedback; board and hub provide non-discretionary exclusivity constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Atomic claim/rejection, duplicate proposal filter, dependency reservation, shared chat/notes, mid-turn inbox and non-force-push shared branch. - Closure path: Worker A claims/changes a task → worker B sees refused claim, chat mention or changed hub → B selects another task, coordinates boundaries or rebases → new board/branch state is visible to both. - Boundary reachability: Multiple workers and board tools are the normal Colony runtime, not an external application constructing its own regulator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Locks and rebases alone are automatic S2 support; positive attribution is limited to model-owned choice to replan, coordinate and repair after those feedback signals. - Evidence: prompts.rs#L46-L75, board.rs#L391-L562, inbox.rs, hub.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high; the code establishes the regulation loop, not conflict-recovery success rates. - Caveats: When only one worker is started, cross-worker S2 is dormant; low-level locking itself is not counted as agent autonomy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: Every worker reads the shared board with all open/claimed/done tasks and the common goal, rather than merely its own tool log. - Current-control decision scope: Any worker can propose missing goal work, amend present task dependencies and commitments, reopen failed completion, or close the whole goal after all current tasks finish and configured checks pass. - Function: Distribute current whole-goal task commitments and regulate unresolved work across the shared colony. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Missing goal subtasks, blocked critical paths, stale completed work, all-done-but-goal-incomplete conditions and changed availability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: From a whole-board current view, propose a 5–15-task plan, add missing work, pick/reassign one&#39;s commitment, reopen a flawed completed task, and finally declare goal completion. - Decision owner: Distributed model workers; one agent initially takes the planning task but any worker may amend the plan or exercise whole-goal closure. No separate central director exists. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: boardview, shared goal, task dependencies/status, board wakeups, one-planning-task bootstrap, goalcomplete eligibility and optional cached verifier. - Closure path: Goal/current board → worker identifies missing or blocked work → proposes/reopens and changes commitments → colleagues see the revised board and act → terminal whole-goal verification/closure. - Boundary reachability: The shipped standard protocol initializes the planning task and gives every worker whole-board tools and goal-completion rights. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The status checks are deterministic, but agents decide priorities, propose new current work and judge missing completeness; S3 is distributed rather than embodied by a manager process. - Evidence: prompts.rs#L46-L63, prompts.rs#L89-L99, mcp.rs#L188-L204, board.rs#L852-L890. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; no measured claim of effective governance across arbitrary worker counts. - Caveats: No separately privileged S3 agent; the same workers own S1 and distributed S3 choices. Operator-goal changes and budgets remain external.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Claim being audited: A peer&#39;s done task or final shared-branch state meets its acceptance criteria and the colony goal. - Ordinary reporting path: Task owner writes taskcomplete summary with a merged commit; status appears on the board. - Complementary access path: Another worker can inspect the actual shared branch and task details, execute checks, then call taskreopen with a reason; configured verify independently executes an operator-specified test command against the current hub commit. - Independence boundary: Source access and separate peer model process are available but independent reviewer assignment is optional and not enforced; automated verify is a check, not a separate agent judgment. - Who acts on findings: Reopened task becomes claimable and workers are signaled; a failed verify refuses goalcomplete, announces the failure and requires branch repair. - Function: Conditional peer challenge of task completion and reality check of the merged goal state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Misleading done summaries, broken peer changes and unverified whole-goal completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A peer can reject a completion with evidence; optional verifier can block a false goal-complete declaration. - Decision owner: A distinct worker when it undertakes peer inspection; test-command outcome and close-goal gate are deterministic. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: taskreopen, source/branch access, task logs, peer notifications, verify commit cache and closure refusal. - Closure path: Owner&#39;s done claim → separate worker inspects actual artifact → reasoned reopen → peer correction/new claim → fresh completion; optional test failure triggers the same operational repair path. - Boundary reachability: Peer-reopen tool and prompt are standard; a different reviewer agent and configured verify command are possible but not required on every completion, warranting C rather than A. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The peer can make a substantive source-grounded audit decision; generic testing/board messages are not themselves independent S3 agency. - Evidence: prompts.rs#L89-L99, board.rs#L692-L719, mcp.rs#L269-L285, mcp.rs#L420-L445. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium for conditional peer pathway; independent-review coverage not established. - Caveats: taskreopen can be used by the original worker; no source code mandates a distinct auditor. A test failure and its deterministic gate do not prove auditor independence.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No evidenced distinct prospective external scanning and organization-level capability adaptation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Current goal implementation, API usage and peer failures, not an evolving future external environment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose and propagate forward-looking capability/strategy shifts in response to environmental futures; absent. - Decision owner: Operator sets goals, worker count, tools and usage limits; workers can revise within-goal plans only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan task T1, peer notes, repeated sessions, usage-limit throttling and dynamic current tasks. - Closure path: Within-goal task replanning returns to present operations; no prospective option evaluation leading to durable new capability and authorized integration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Choosing tomorrow&#39;s step of an already fixed software goal is current execution, not S4 environmental adaptation. - Evidence: prompts.rs#L38-L102, supervisor.rs#L1-L14. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for reviewed first-party modes. - Caveats: A user may assign a future-oriented project goal; task subject matter does not establish a distinct harness S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No harness-owned identity/ultimate-policy governance that can legitimately redefine the binding goal and return a constitutional decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User-configured budget, deadlines, permissions and Git restrictions are operational bounds, not identity disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Resolve binding-purpose/policy conflicts with authorized parent return; absent. - Decision owner: Operator supplies goal and budget, Rust supervisor enforces stops, and workers choose within those bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Goal text, usage caps, task/branch gates, deterministic verifier and human external CLI configuration. - Closure path: Worker goal-complete stops the job and budget stop cancels workers; neither authorizes a change of organizational identity. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A worker&#39;s finishing declaration is current objective closure (S3), not binding S5 purpose adjudication. - Evidence: supervisor.rs, mcp.rs#L420-L445, prompts.rs#L38-L46. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Operator authorization can govern the colony from outside the reviewed first-party system; no internal legitimate parent role is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/387aebd9a81afa0b50514e12aa1899cc9771776d/assessments/co/colony.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 387aebd9a81afa0b50514e12aa1899cc9771776d\">387aebd &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T16:05:09+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MattGrundy/leadkit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Leadkit</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MattGrundy/leadkit/tree/3efcde576a5f687b0013fad902fe9cff8be11766\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3efcde576a5f687b0013fad902fe9cff8be11766\">3efcde5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement project-scoped coding and verification tasks through agent-selected reading, editing, testing and tool use. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variable code requests, test outcomes, repository content and task-specific errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the installed developer/specialist Claude Code Agent decides how to perform its assigned task and reacts to tool observations and reviewer feedback. - Decision owner: active model-driven role Agent in Claude Code Teams, configured by Leadkit&#39;s shipped first-party role definitions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claude Code model/tool host, installed role allowlist, task files, path guard, queue/status verbs and project profile. - Closure path: tools return output to the active agent; it changes/tests code, records evidence and returns its result to task/reviewer queue. - Boundary reachability: standard adoption installs the role definitions and wires hooks; the deployed Host Agent Teams directly consumes these, whereas bare leadkit CLI-only execution is explicitly not claimed to be autonomous. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the role agent chooses substantive code edits and validation steps; CLI/guards enforce boundaries and do not generate the operational choices. - Evidence: internal/starter/agents/developer.md#L1-L40; internal/starter/agents/lead.md#L1-L42; internal/starter/starter.go#L1-L85; docs/ADOPTING-LEADKIT.md#L1-L105. - Basis: explicit / structural within adopted supported mode. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: model execution belongs to external Claude Code; no live paid Claude Code Teams E2E was performed, and disabled Teams yields no autonomous team.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: lead-assigned developer and specialist Agents working potentially overlapping git paths and successive reviews, each with its own task/owner identity. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent edits to the same path can silently undo peer work; premature parallel critique can review a SHA invalidated by prior code-review fixes; orphaned task ownership can stall delivery. - Attenuating coordination relation: lead Agent deliberately assigns path ownership, sequences the code-review and critique sessions, returns failures to original owner, and orchestrator Agent resolves stale/idle queue handoffs. Path/queue and gate checks enforce these decisions. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: reviewer findings change the owner&#39;s next implementation, a changed commit invalidates old approval, and queue/orphan status causes reassignment or escalation rather than duplicate work. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: these mechanisms target concrete shared-path collision, stale-SHA review race and lost-ownership hazards and return corrective outcomes to specific work units. - Function: attenuate interference among parallel project Agents so ownership and review outcomes remain consistent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: shared-file contention, role drift, stale review race, lost work and duplicate ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Lead chooses paths and reviewer order; the orchestrator chooses how to handle queue stalls within documented roles; deterministic planner provides constraints and signals. - Decision owner: model-driven Lead/Orchestrator Agents in the shipped enabled-team operating mode, not the Go planner or filesystem alone. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ownership guard, role lanes, task IDs, deterministic plan, reviewer chain SHA binding and distinct identities. - Closure path: owner assignment enters task store/queue, agents act within scoped paths, review findings return to builder, and next verified SHA advances review/ship stages. - Boundary reachability: these roles and paths are installed through the public adoption workflow and use active Claude Code Teams Agent/SendMessage capabilities, not only project maintainer dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the meaningful assignment and remediation selection belongs to autonomous role Agents; guards deny inconsistent actions but do not choose the route. - Evidence: internal/starter/agents/lead.md#L1-L42; internal/starter/agents/orchestrator.md#L1-L28; internal/starter/team-protocol.md#L1-L100; internal/orchestrate/plan.go#L1-L130; internal/gate/chain.go#L65-L140. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: medium-high for standard deployed role protocol. - Caveats: coordination relies on following first-party role instructions; the Go CLI does not itself launch or monitor a proprietary model session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Whole-system current view: project queue/status/owner/priority/dependency records plus live roster passed in from Agent Teams; account burn telemetry, workstream count, budget reserve and pause-lock status. - Current-control decision scope: choose active team workload and concurrency, defer dispatch when budget is stressed, prioritize critical tasks, requeue orphaned work or grant owner-directed staffing/priority changes. - Function: maintain present project-team commitments, workload and throughput within user resource and authority bounds. - Disturbance / variety regulated: misallocated role capacity, budget burn, stuck tasks and competing current priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party planner exposes S3-specific route/requeue/hold/triage choices, while the installed autonomous actor must assemble a live roster and act on higher-level capacity/priority reports; in the parent mode the human selects the active roster/priority/parallelism and authorizes changes. - Decision owner: C constructor has deterministic CLI and Agent-facing role protocol but lacks a self-contained autonomous S3 governor with all decisive rights; P mode is the legitimate project operator who decides and applies current resource/priority constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: leadkit status, burn/priority planner, usage history, account pause lock, role roster, task queue, apply confirmation, waker/sweep daemons. - Closure path: computed route/hold/escalation is returned to orchestrator or human; accepted priority/roster/budget decisions alter task status or available consumers, and subsequent queue/burn samples expose the result. - Boundary reachability: CLI+roles+hooks/daemons are standard installed interfaces, including explicit owner changes, not private governance; C still needs external Agent Teams and operator adoption, P needs actual human choice. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: consuming a deterministic burn verdict and routing plan does not by itself make the autonomous Lead own a whole-system capacity tradeoff. Parent control is a distinct explicit operating mode rather than a decoration on an approval button. - Evidence: internal/orchestrate/plan.go#L1-L130; internal/orchestrate/apply.go#L1-L80; cmd/leadkit/cmdorchestrate.go#L1-L110; internal/burn/burn.go#L1-L115; internal/burn/throttle.go#L1-L70; docs/ADOPTING-LEADKIT.md#L20-L105. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: medium; project-specific actual authorization and agent roster are deployment-specific. - Caveats: automatic pause/resume is deterministic enforcement; the C notation is restricted to S3-specific live-resource decision paths, not arbitrary queue commands. Parent mode presumes a real operator uses reports to revise priority/roster rather than merely installing a static config.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: a code diff at a pinned SHA is correct/safe and actually serves the ticket&#39;s intended goal. - Ordinary reporting path: builder Agent&#39;s completion claim, code changes and test logs. - Complementary access path: separate read-only code-review and critique Agents inspect diff, task and surrounding code under different questions and record own SHA-bound verdicts/evidence. - Independence boundary: distinct installed roles/tools (reviewers lack Edit/Write/SendMessage), order and different-role gate checks; reviewers are not the builder and may not file the other layer&#39;s verdict. - Who acts on findings: original builder/lead receives changes-needed; gate blocks push/PR path until matching independent ready layers, then finalise/human may proceed. - Function: complementary audit of operational output and purpose before a consequential code shipment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: self-certified implementation errors, wrong goal, stale-SHA approval, and review-role collusion/impersonation accidents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate model-driven reviewer Agents make substantive correctness and goal-fit judgments, each recorded on observed commit evidence. - Decision owner: reviewer Agents own their respective judgments, while Go gate and forge enforcement decide only whether required current, role-separated records exist. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewer read-only grants, mutation-evidence notes, chain/order, SHA stamps, status/queue, push hooks and optional verified server protection. - Closure path: changes-needed returns work to developer, revised SHA invalidates old approvals, both ready verdicts permit finalise/push within granted authority. - Boundary reachability: reviewer roles and gate are shipped/installable first-party components in the normal team setup, not merely CI tests; the external Claude Code host executes reviewers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: semantic findings belong to independent review Agents; checking SHA/order/identity and command gating is deterministic enforcement. - Evidence: internal/starter/agents/code-review-agent.md#L1-L65; internal/starter/agents/critique-agent.md#L1-L85; internal/gate/chain.go#L1-L140; internal/starter/team-protocol.md#L35-L100; cmd/leadkit/cmdgate.go#L1-L95. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: medium-high for first-party role separation and gate. - Caveats: recorded --by identity may be self-asserted when host does not bind CLAUDEAGENTNAME; local hooks are bypassable without forge protection. Multiple reviewers may share provider/model family; independence is role/evidence separation, not externally certified audit.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no evidenced external-future sensing, adaptation-option judgment and return into current project capability at the assessed boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: emerging technology/organizational threats or future opportunities are not a first-party recurrent object of control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party S4 actor chooses strategic redesign from external signals and deploys it. - Decision owner: not established; humans may modify roles or open issues; normal Lead coordination operates on present tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: feedback-as-issues in overarching-lead protocol, role capture, postmortems, repo governance and backlog. - Closure path: issues preserve potential improvements but no closed strategic adoption loop into team capabilities is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning future tasks and capturing lessons is not necessarily outside-and-then adaptation. - Evidence: internal/starter/agents/overarching-lead.md#L1-L110; internal/starter/starter.go#L1-L100; README.md#L75-L145. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the user can commission tool upgrades; they remain discretionary external work, not a shipped S4 mode. ### Absence scope\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: whether a project team may grant itself standing rights to push, create PRs, merge or modify protected repository policy, versus retaining those fundamental authorization rights at the repository owner. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: direct project/repository owner alone; peer lead and overarching coordinator cannot grant or relay authority in lieu of the owner. - Return-to-operation path: owner-approved authority.&lt;repo&gt; entry in the project profile changes guards and governed PR/push commands; protected-branch rules can be installed and read back, and subsequent operations are permitted/blocked by the returned grant. - Function: reserve ultimate scope of consequential agent action to direct human owner authority and apply it as a durable team constitution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: agent self-grant, laundering authority through a peer coordinator, unreviewed merges/pushes and inconsistent rights across repositories. - Decisive decision or feedback right: grant or deny standing repository mutation/merge authority, and amend protected-branch policy under owner mandate. - Decision owner: legitimate human project/repository owner in the shipped parent-governed setup, not the autonomous Lead or other session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-repo authority profile, adoption stop point, PR/push guard, default-deny allowlists, forge protection verification and doctor. - Closure path: team must request direct owner grant; owner decision is written to profile/forge policy; subsequent hook/CLI operations consume it, permit only granted action and report denials. Denial leaves default-deny intact. - Boundary reachability: authority-grant step and policy guards are part of normal adoption flow and CLI; code explicitly reads named per-repo authority, so the mode is real without relying on GitHub contributors as a metasystem. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents cannot legitimately establish their own standing constitutional authority merely by relaying a request; the owner remains the sole source of grants, while Go enforces the returned decision. - Evidence: docs/ADOPTING-LEADKIT.md#L20-L105; internal/starter/agents/overarching-lead.md#L1-L110; README.md#L75-L145; docs/design/agent-sandboxing-and-enforcement.md#L1-L95; cmd/leadkit/cmdgate.go#L1-L95. - Basis: explicit / structural parent-governed closure. - Confidence: medium-high for first-party policy path, not for an unseen specific live owner decision. - Caveats: ordinary per-PR human review is not counted as S5; standing authority is the constitutional object. Client-side hooks alone are bypassable; host/forge enforcement and real owner-supplied profile must be configured.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a4ff63c7225d233941c9c33cd63d66584ef61ae4/assessments/le/leadkit.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a4ff63c7225d233941c9c33cd63d66584ef61ae4\">a4ff63c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-13T22:24:43+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/didi/helixharness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HelixHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-13\">2026-11-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/didi/helixharness/tree/8b23069fb464104ce556798ec69737f7eba469b3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8b23069fb464104ce556798ec69737f7eba469b3\">8b23069 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Host-agent-directed specification, implementation, testing and archiving of a concrete software feature. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing requirements, repository code/constraints, failing tests, changed review scope and incomplete task evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Host coding agent chooses feature decomposition, implementation edits, tests and review remediation within the loaded first-party workflow. - Decision owner: Codex/Claude Code agent executing shipped HelixHarness command/workflow as a supported host plugin mode; human supplies objectives and special decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Workflow router, Spec/Tasks state, lifecycle scripts, validation receipts, knowledge/context loading, TDD hooks and evidence ledger. - Closure path: Propose produces Spec/Tasks → Apply model edits code and gets tests/reviewer feedback → repair or human decision → Archive seals verified task and relevant knowledge for later features. - Boundary reachability: Public plugin commands bind to source-shipped single/workspace workflows and execute within supported Codex/Claude Code sessions; external model host supplies reasoning but first-party plugin instantiates task-specific role/procedure and closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the coding model leaves deterministic stage/receipt validators, not an owner that can implement new functionality or choose issue-specific code changes. - Evidence: helixharness/commands/propose.md, helixharness/commands/apply.md, helixharness/workflows/single/apply.md, helixharness/scripts/workflowrouter.py, docs/user-guide.md - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Coordinate distinct repository S1 workers in a multi-repository Workspace according to dependency and exclusive ownership constraints. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel repo changes can overlap ownership, consume outputs in incorrect order or race parent edits. - Distinct S1 units: Independent per-repository RepoJobs with agent-owned Propose/Apply/Archive work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: A downstream service depends on a not-yet-ready upstream service; two workers writing the same repository would collide. - Attenuating coordination relation: Workspace Coordinator constructs a dependency-aware wave, launches isolated RepoJobs with single owner per repo, waits for native terminal results and recomputes plan on validated completion or blocker. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Failure/needsinput blocks dependent successors; successful independent completion checks enable eligible following RepoJobs and update the root feature state. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The documented single-owner, dependency/forbidden-scope and validated-return rules regulate real interference between concurrent S1 code units; source scripts reject dependency cycles. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-operated Workspace parent selects valid dispatches and revises work frontier from source-verified job outcomes. - Decision owner: First-party Workspace Coordinator role instantiated in supported coding-agent host; deterministic DAG/validation enforces bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Workspace registry, RepoJob contract, dependency DAG, host-native spawn/wait mapping, read/write scopes and independent completioncheck. - Closure path: Root Spec+repo graph → Coordinator waves → repo-level agent work → native terminal and source check → reconstructed plan changes later S1 dispatch. - Boundary reachability: Shipped workspace workflow and scripts create jobs and host-contract mapping, requiring a supported host-native subagent facility or documented serial unavailable fallback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static DAG sorting alone is not S2 autonomy; the bounded coordinating host agent handles actual job assignment, agent callbacks and failed dependencies. - Evidence: helixharness/reference/workspace-repo-dispatch.md, helixharness/scripts/workspace.py, helixharness/reference/subagent-runtime-protocol.md, docs/user-guide.md - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Parent oversight of current project/Workspace commitments and framework evaluation resources. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Blocked features, incomplete review evidence, mismatched dependency progress, evaluation capacity and budget contention. - Whole-system current view: Status lists active feature progress, blockers, dependencies and next steps across Workspace; optional platform shows evaluation runs, worker slots and budgets. - Current-control decision scope: Human selects which feature to continue/repair, grants scope/baseline waivers or changes the evaluation Worker pool and resource budgets. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Change present project commitments/resource constraints after examining aggregated state, rather than merely approve one code change. - Decision owner: Human developer/team or framework-maintainer parent using supported status/platform controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Status script, Workspace plan, stage receipts, review gates, platform run/resource configuration. - Closure path: Parent views Workspace/project or platform current progress → chooses change in active work or capacity → plugin workflow or platform settings execute → next jobs/runs use changed commitment. - Boundary reachability: The plugin&#39;s status and task-update commands are shipped; optional self-hosted platform has documented UI/config and Workers. Human intervention is explicit rather than a claimed autonomous S3 manager. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: No supported agent autonomously owns allocation of the entire team&#39;s resource commitments; scripts/queues enforce the human&#39;s choices. - Evidence: helixharness/commands/status.md, helixharness/scripts/projectstatus.py, helixharness/scripts/workspace.py, docs/user-guide.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Single-feature approvals alone would not suffice; credited P concerns whole-Workspace/project status and current intervention. The optional platform has a distinct maintainer operating focus.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independent first-party code review of S1 implementation claims against direct diff, project Standards and requested Spec. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Implementer may assert tests passed while code violates standards or misses requirements. - Claim being audited: That the Apply implementation satisfies the feature Spec and repository engineering constraints. - Ordinary reporting path: Implementer completion, tests, Tasks state and verification artifacts. - Complementary access path: Frozen Review Bundle of actual git diff/working-tree scope, Spec, source Standards and independent test result; two read-only reviewer agents independently inspect the code. - Independence boundary: Standards and Spec agents are separately spawned in isolated contexts, read-only, never the Implementer context; unavailable host isolation yields BLOCKED, not self-review. - Who acts on findings: Implementer repairs MUSTFIX and runs new complete review; DECISIONNEEDED returns to human; only separate APPROVE axes enable Evidence and Archive. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Each independent reviewer agent issues reasoned evidence-backed verdict on its own axis; parent workflow accepts/rejects changes and repeats based on returned findings. - Decision owner: Two dedicated host-native subagent review instances instantiated by the first-party code-review skill; neither implementer nor deterministic checker supplies their discretionary verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Review fixed point, frozen git snapshot, source-scope hash, reviewer role contract, separate verdict logs and evidence receipts. - Closure path: Implementation → frozen actual-code evidence → independent Standards/Spec judgments → targeted repair or allowed continuation → rerun tests and review → accepted/blocked archive path. - Boundary reachability: Apply requires the shipped code-review skill and true native parallel isolated reviewers in a supported host; missing capability blocks instead of faking independence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic scope and tests are support; two distinct review agents judge actual implementation vs independent evidence and return corrective findings. - Evidence: helixharness/skills/code-review/SKILL.md, helixharness/workflows/single/apply.md, helixharness/reference/subagent-runtime-protocol.md, helixharness/scripts/workflow.py - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: Construct a prospective framework-improvement cycle from external usage and benchmark evidence, not just current user coding work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing usage pain points, rework, capability regressions and new framework requirements outside an individual feature task. - External distinction: Usage ledger and linked telemetry from application repositories plus controlled version/Case evaluation results. - Future / prospective distinction: Improvement hypotheses and candidate commits are about what future HelixHarness framework capability should change, rather than finishing the current feature. - Adaptation option generated: Platform evolution analyzer proposes an evidence-linked hypothesis, candidate builder constructs an isolated change, and evaluation compares frozen Base/Candidate Cases. - Path back into current capability / S3: A candidate/version and its verdict are presented to a maintainer for a separate decision to publish/adopt into live plugin; the first-party platform stops short of autonomous or parent-complete production integration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Prospective option formation/evaluation is first-party and model-supported, but enduring framework adoption remains a separately composed maintainer/git workflow. - Decision owner: First-party evolution analyzer and candidate builder provide an S4-specific constructor; final source publication owner must be supplied outside this pipeline. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Telemetry consent, evidence eligibility, capability catalog, frozen Case/Run, separate Workers, scheduler and evaluated candidate ref. - Closure path: Usage/evaluation evidence → hypothesis → candidate commit → matched independent evaluation/verdict → proposed capability decision handoff; external maintainer must complete integration. - Boundary reachability: Optional installed platform exposes EvolutionService, scheduler and worker pool with documented configurations; not an imagined future extension, though disabled by default. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Option generation can be agent-driven but effective published-capability adaptation is not autonomously closed by the platform; C preserves this missing final composition. - Evidence: helixharness-platform/evolution/service.py, helixharness-platform/evolution/candidate.py, helixharness-platform/evolution/scheduler.py, docs/user-guide.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Default scheduler off; matching calibrated Cases and configured model/Workers are required. Clicking Adopt enters an experiment, not automatic publication to live plugin. No provider-backed evolution run verified.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Parent-governed team engineering mission/constraints and legitimate boundaries of what the coding harness may do. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting requests against team rules, changes to accepted knowledge, unauthorized work scopes and decisions to waive project constraints. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Which engineering principles, accepted specification, workflow rights and durable project knowledge govern the agent across tasks. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Human project owner sets binding Harness/project constraints, chooses feature scope and can confirm or refuse adding knowledge; agent cannot unilaterally redefine the enduring charter. - Return-to-operation path: Owner-approved Constraints/Spec/Knowledge documents and explicit decision receipts are persisted into application repository; later Propose/Apply reads applicable indexes and constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human maintains the standing project identity/charter and decides material changes to it rather than ordinary model-level implementation choices. - Decision owner: Project owner/team parent in the supported supervised plugin mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Document loading protocol, Spec/Tasks receipts, archive acceptance, stage/decision gates, host permissions and versioned knowledge indexes. - Closure path: Policy-level project issue → owner decision on binding requirement or durable Knowledge → accepted repository artifact → later agent workflow loads it as normative constraint and alters decisions. - Boundary reachability: The plugin&#39;s Init/Propose/Archive workflows read and write these installed project constraints; explicit owner approval gates remain part of normal supported mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent implements within frozen constraints; it does not hold legitimate ultimate authority to waive or rewrite the team&#39;s standing engineering charter. - Evidence: helixharness/reference/loading-protocol.md, helixharness/workflows/single/archive.md, helixharness/workflows/single/propose.md, docs/user-guide.md - Basis: structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/50134598044ffb2248f4663fe0df5eca0f7b30ab/assessments/he/helixharness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 50134598044ffb2248f4663fe0df5eca0f7b30ab\">5013459 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T18:09:58+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ebetica/recurse\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Recurse</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ebetica/recurse/tree/6e4d231e299a14f19fe6eee9d9f7f43781780c2a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6e4d231e299a14f19fe6eee9d9f7f43781780c2a\">6e4d231 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce code/repository outcomes through independently model-directed persistent work on a task, including observation, edits, tests, terminal commands and iteration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing code state, command/test failures, incomplete context, long-running commands and interruptions/restarts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each agent chooses its subsequent tools, edits, tests, recovery, further child tasks or final result from observed feedback. - Decision owner: the Pi Durable model actor in each root/child conversation, subject to external operator authorization and safety-tool constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-agent AgentNode, Durable Harness, background shell/wait/monitor, persisted messages, working-directory state and safety checks. - Closure path: operator/parent task → model-selected tools/edits → shell/file/agent observations and completion wakes → subsequent model decision and result report; durable work can resume across browser disconnection and restart. - Boundary reachability: normal recurse /path starts a real agent; documented subagent create starts more independent model agents from CLI without a special development fixture. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model turns choose task-level actions and responses; deterministic process supervision only transports results and gates execution. - Evidence: quickstart and purpose, runtime guide, Durable turns. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: two or more independently running implementer child agents with separate model turns, local tasks and repository-editing decisions, under one parent tree. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous implementation in one checkout can overwrite, interleave or destabilize another child&#39;s in-progress file changes; the official guide explicitly motivates separate worktrees to prevent this shared-write interference. - Attenuating coordination relation: the parent model assigns separate named managed Git worktrees during subagent create --worktree, so concurrent implementers operate against isolated working files and branches; parent/worker exchange and eventual review/merge reconcile outcomes rather than expose the same working tree to competing writers. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the chosen worktree path becomes the spawned child&#39;s effective working directory for its later tool calls; its branch or PR comes back as an integration artifact, and the parent can message/reassign a child or defer/alter a merge when work overlaps. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the intervention targets a named code-edit collision between distinct S1 producers; it changes their actual workspace topology and future write targets. Messaging alone, ordinary task splitting or generic queueing is not credited. - Function: damp parallel implementation interference by deciding and maintaining task-specific checkout separation and integrating branch-level results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent editing races and inadvertent shared-workspace corruption; downstream logical Git merge conflicts remain possible. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which child receives a fresh checkout and task/branch, and adjust work allocation or subsequent merge handling after reports. - Decision owner: parent model-led agent when operating the documented parallel-worktree mode; deterministic Git/worktree holder machinery implements the isolation decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagent.ts --worktree, worktree.ts named checkouts and holder claims, per-agent cwd, Git branches, child messages/final reports. - Closure path: parent recognizes parallel edits → assigns separate worktrees → each child modifies its own checkout → branch/PR or feedback returns → parent selects review, integration, clarification or further task allocation; future child work remains separated. - Boundary reachability: the shipped CLI and runtime support worktree-bearing spawns; the first-party guide explicitly recommends and illustrates this normal multi-agent delivery pattern, not an unimplemented suggestion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent model elects when/how to separate work and which tasks/branches to assign; static worktree creation, holder bookkeeping and Git merge mechanics are enforcement, not autonomous coordinators. - Evidence: worktrees, multiagent and collision purpose, child creation interface, managed checkouts and claims, example agent graph. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: there is no automatic semantic merge-conflict arbitrator; S2 here is model-selected preventive checkout isolation and returned branch handling, not guaranteed conflict-free integration. One-agent or shared-cwd use does not exercise it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: the parent&#39;s model-request snapshot lists its child/descendant status and queued inputs, while native subagent reads/waits/messages expose individual work and results; the supervisor maintains authoritative live membership and lifecycle status. - Current-control decision scope: the parent can choose/constrain concurrent commitments and effort on creation, interrupt or redirect active children, reassign with move, retire stale work cells and integrate or rework their output as the joint goal demands. - Function: govern a coding tree&#39;s ongoing resource/commitment allocation and exceptional intervention while independent implementers own their local tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled agents, poor distribution of effort, late user requirements, active conflicts or incomplete child tasks and divergent branch outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose whether to launch, interrupt, redirect, reparent or retire child work and whether its returned work satisfies the whole task. - Decision owner: model-backed parent agent in the supported multiagent tree; operator may intervene or take over, but operator interaction is not assumed to constitute an independent whole-team S3 parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: live Supervisor/tree projection, agent digest, subagent.ts create/send/interrupt/wait/move/rm, Durable report holds and completion delivery, fixed resource/process limits. - Closure path: status/child output/monitor/user steer → parent sees current tree information → parent selects task or resource intervention → supervisor/CLI changes relevant child agent and sends new input → changed work and updated status return to parent. - Boundary reachability: the agent prompt includes a live tree digest; the shipped CLI supports the control operations under normal local execution, and child reports reach the parent automatically. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent model decides interventions and current tradeoffs; the supervisor enforces ownership/fencing and routes commands but is not itself a discretionary S3 agent. Merely having a class named Supervisor is insufficient. - Evidence: parent model tree digest, CLI control surface, tree ownership and interventions, child report closure. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party standard operating audit process is evidenced that independently inspects completed operational results and returns findings to current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: optimistic code-completion claims, faulty edits or unsafe commands could require independent checking. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect raw delivered artifacts outside ordinary child reporting and issue a complementary verdict with corrective return; no installed closed path established. - Decision owner: pi-safety uses a separate model to grant/deny proposed actions; an optional reviewer child can be authored by a user, but there is no default complementary outcome auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate model safety reviewer, action transcript, built-in denial gate, recipe for a review-agent graph, source tests and developer review rules. - Closure path: safety&#39;s pre-execution DENY returns a reason and can change the next actor proposal; this regulates permissions, not independent observation of the action&#39;s real outcome. User-authored review scripts may create further loops but are not established here as an active standard process. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the second model genuinely judges proposed tool authorization, but its evidence deliberately excludes tool outputs, edited file content and post-execution result; its separate model identity does not make it complementary operational audit. - Evidence: review evidence boundary and fail-open behavior, user-composed reviewer example, agent report path. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no distinct prospective environment-model and capability adaptation loop is established beyond ordinary coding tasks and event-driven continuation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing provider/tool ecosystems, CI trends, new project requirements and future architectural possibilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose a new whole-harness capability based on external/future distinctions and carry it back into live operation; not evidenced. - Decision owner: agents can investigate and change repository code when tasked, while users/developers configure tools and skills; no autonomous organization-level S4 owner is shipped. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: background monitors, memory, MCP configuration, skills, models and task-local planning. - Closure path: a CI/deployment monitor can wake an agent for immediate handling, but a signal-to-task reaction is not itself prospective option formation and a capability-adaptation return to S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning future code steps is S1 task choice; Recurse&#39;s extensibility offers means to construct S4 but no first-party defined future/environment adaptation decision loop. - Evidence: monitor and goals examples, extensibility principles, runtime guide. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudicator is established for the multiagent coding organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risky commands, operator intent conflicts, policy exceptions, agent takeovers and security-mode choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: settle a whole-organization identity or ultimate-policy tension and return a binding new rule; no closed function-specific path shown. - Decision owner: operator/developer owns Recurse permissions and safety instructions; model safety reviewer applies predefined action policy, and child/parent agents follow ordinary delegated task authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user task prompts, first-party pi-safety and custom additive policy rules, operator-only takeover, tool denial circuit breaker and user consent for exact actions. - Closure path: model reviewer may return DENY and request a safer action or explicit authorization; that decides an individual command under existing policy, not a new identity-governing rule for the organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model can reason about dangerous operations, but ultimate authority to change policy is not delegated to a live S5 body with a policy-level issue/escalation and operational return. - Evidence: safety review limits, operator takeover, takeover state/authority. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e94effaef30205f31fb915634491cbab971ca89d/assessments/re/recurse.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e94effaef30205f31fb915634491cbab971ca89d\">e94effa &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T18:07:29+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CJVigil/Orco\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Orco</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CJVigil/Orco/tree/148f21d5a4dcf4f833e87d9fa38d3a1bfbe9df59\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 148f21d5a4dcf4f833e87d9fa38d3a1bfbe9df59\">148f21d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform substantive software/project tasks through configured model-bearing connected agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different user requirements, source code state, failing tool results, changed ticket context and incomplete information. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose investigations, tool invocations, file modifications, responses, task completion and when to ask for input. - Decision owner: Connected model agent using the launched CLI and MCP tools; the human initiating work owns the original request, not the agent&#39;s operational choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task claim leases, connected bridge, model CLI adapter, prompt/context builder, local workspace, MCP task state and policy/budget gates. - Closure path: User/other agent requests work → bridge claims task and launches model → model reads context/uses tools and adjusts work to observed results → updatetask done/blocked/failed updates shared state. - Boundary reachability: The shipped orco connect bridge actually starts configured local models from assigned tasks; the default bridge prompt and installed Claude-code skill prescribe this task-to-action lifecycle, rather than relying on the maintainer&#39;s development agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic orchestration delivers requests and enforces limits; the model chooses substantive work under those limits. - Evidence: bridge default/runner, model adapter, task API. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high for wired agent decision path; medium for behavior of the external model runtime. - Caveats: No live external model credentials or end-to-end provider sessions were exercised in this review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Prevent parallel connected operational agents from modifying an incompatible shared resource at the same time, with feedback for changed agent behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two simultaneously working agents both attempt to use the same branch, deployment target, migration or another named shared project resource. - Decisive decision or feedback right: On resource conflict, choose wait, release/retry later or pick another task; decide when the shared resource can be relinquished. - Decision owner: The involved model-bearing task agents in the shipped bridge prompt / integration skill. Deterministic lock service owns exclusion and fencing, not the discretionary response to denied access. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Project-scoped named lock holder, TTL, monotonically advanced fence, conflict response identifying holder and expiry, blocking wait helper and explicit acquire/release MCP tools. - Closure path: Agent A obtains named lock → agent B sees conflict identifying holder/expiry → shipped model guidance makes B wait or change work rather than collide → lock expiry/release and subsequent acquire permit resumed work. - Boundary reachability: The bridge&#39;s bundled DEFAULTPROMPT explicitly requires acquirelock/releaselock before touching shared resources; the distributed Claude-code Orco skill explicitly specifies waiting or picking different work after a conflict. Both are provided for ordinary supported agent connections. - Distinct S1 units: Separate connected coding agents with independent task assignments and local model processes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Simultaneous work on a single branch, deployment target or migration. - Attenuating coordination relation: Mutual-exclusion leases plus a returned lock-conflict signal that the model uses to alter its next activity. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The denied model changes task/order or waits until the holder releases, so following tool actions use a nonconflicting shared resource. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The named resource collision is concrete inter-agent interference; first-party lock acquisition, conflict disclosure and instructed behavioral change close a specific attenuation cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Lock arithmetic and fencing are runtime enforcement, whereas agents choose the amended work schedule after feedback. - Evidence: locks, bridge prompt, installed Orco skill. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high for standard connected agents and actual conflict return. - Caveats: Lock discipline is advisory for code execution outside Orco; a noncompliant agent can bypass these project-scoped locks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: View current work commitments and failures across managed agents/projects and change live allocation, task priorities and intervention decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stalled agent tasks, blocked tickets, overlapping priorities, waiting approvals, CI failure, overspend and missed current commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide which current work to reprioritize, pause, assign, unblock or escalate in light of portfolio/project state. - Decision owner: Base constructor mode: a separately configured model supervisor asked by the operator to use the shipped projectplan, listtickets and updateticket tools (C); no such supervisor is installed by default. Parent mode: authenticated human org/project administrator viewing portfolio health and changing tickets/pauses/budgets (P). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Server-generated project plan and ticket summaries, cross-project health/roadmap/CI/approval/budget aggregation, ticket mutation API, scoped pause/budget control and audit events. - Closure path: Base: constructed model supervisory session reads whole project open work and plan → decides reassignment/priority update → first-party ticket edits change connected agents&#39; subsequent commitments. Parent: portfolio displays blocked/stalled/over-budget agents and tasks → admin decides assignment/priority/pause → persisted controls/tickets alter later bridge task starts and available work. - Boundary reachability: First-party MCP ships the necessary project-wide read/update tools for a constructor-configured agent mode, while the first-party Orco human UI and controls route expose portfolio current state and authorized admin changes in normal self-hosted deployment; neither requires maintainer CI or a private service. - Whole-system current view: For the base constructor, the accessible plan, tickets and dependencies of the selected Orco project (not a complete cross-instance view); for the parent, Portfolio.overview aggregates all visible projects&#39; tickets, stalled agents, approvals, CI, budgets and milestone risk. - Current-control decision scope: Assigning/updating task commitments, priority and workflow status, pausing selected agents/projects or all agents, and modifying operational budget limits in response to present problems. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The runtime computes health and enforces controls but does not select a supervisory response. An operator must construct an agent manager to own that discretionary S3 right; the shipped human-admin mode closes it without an autonomous manager. - Evidence: portfolio, MCP project/ticket actions, tickets, runtime controls. - Basis: structural + explicit - Confidence: medium for constructor eligibility, high for human-admin closure. - Caveats: C does not assert a first-party always-on autonomous supervisor. The portfolio view itself is informational until a legitimate admin/model decision changes work.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: A distinct connected review agent independently evaluates another agent&#39;s concrete work and returns corrective findings. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A coding agent may declare a ticket complete despite defects or missing acceptance requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Review the actual ticket/workspace and choose approved or changesrequested with an explanatory note. - Decision owner: A separately addressed model-bearing reviewer, not the original task agent. Human reviewers are also supported, but not used to upgrade agent ownership. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Requester != reviewer identity check, separate reviewer task and bridge wake, review status persistence, ticket review/rework transition and task restart. - Closure path: Task agent requests review of ticket → separate reviewer agent receives a new explicit task to inspect the work and submit verdict → changesrequested writes review note, moves ticket to rework and starts original agent&#39;s work again; an agent approval records assessment while final done remains gated to a person. - Boundary reachability: Reviews.request creates a real task for a named agent reviewer through first-party Tasks.create, which the shipped connected bridge executes; Reviews.submit directly implements rework and restart. - Claim being audited: Work correctness and acceptance for a particular Orco task/ticket. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker submits updatetask done or ticket progress in its own thread. - Complementary access path: A different agent&#39;s model run has a separate task identity, can read the project ticket/context and inspect actual workspace through its own configured coding CLI. - Independence boundary: Reviewer cannot be the requester; it holds a different Orco participant identity and process. Institutional independence, different model provider and read-only file permissions are not guaranteed. - Who acts on findings: The review subsystem moves the ticket to rework and starts the assigned coding agent again with the review note attached. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A reviewing LLM exercises the verdict; deterministic review/ticket machinery transmits the judgment and restarts work. - Evidence: reviews request, verdict and restart, bridge, ticket review path. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high for optional first-party reviewer mode. - Caveats: Review is not mandatory for every task. A separate agent identity alone does not prove critical rigor or provider independence; review quality is unmeasured here.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No shipped closed cycle that senses external/prospective change, selects adaptation to future capability and returns that selection to current operational repertoire. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future milestone risk, new follow-up work noticed during tasks and recurring job demand are recorded, but these are not independently established as future environment intelligence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge external/future environmental alternatives and adopt a changed capability or operating repertoire; not established. - Decision owner: Humans manage milestones, accept follow-up suggestions and approve schedules; ordinary connected agents can propose work and schedules but do not hold a shipped adaptation mandate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Portfolio cross-project roadmap, dated milestones, agent suggestions with optional owner-authorized ticket filing, scheduled recurring tasks and human approvals. - Closure path: Agent can suggest a follow-up → owner accepts into backlog (or an allowed automatic ticket is filed), and scheduled tasks can later run; no documented installed step converts future outside-environment analysis into a selected capability adaptation that returns to operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Backlog intake, forecast risk labels and repeat execution do not alone imply outside-and-then strategy or a governed capability change. - Evidence: suggestions, schedules, portfolio roadmap, MCP milestone operations. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium, bounded to the shipped first-party standard distribution. - Caveats: A human organization using Orco can implement strategic analysis externally; no universal claim against that larger system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Legitimate human parent authority establishes organization-wide and project-specific enduring operating identity/rules and returns binding decisions to subsequent agent work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting agent freedoms, project-local exceptions, unsafe spending/deploy authority and mismatch between organizational mandate and project execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Define or revise authoritative organizational/project rules, required approvals, allowed operational agents and persistent task-budget policy; subordinate projects may tighten but may not relax org constraints. - Decision owner: Authenticated human organization administrator or project administrator; first-party Policies.set refuses agents and applies scope checks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persisted org/project policy, mergePolicy inheritance/tightening, agent bridge prompt policy lines, new-task admission and budget validation, audit trail and controls event. - Closure path: Admin receives governance need and sets org/project policy → it is persisted and scoped into the effective project mandate → new task creation rejects forbidden agents/excess budgets; later bridge prompts include binding rules and approval requirements → subsequent agent operation reflects the decision. - Boundary reachability: The first-party self-hosted server exposes Policies.set to authenticated administrators and directly calls effective policy at task admission and rulesFor in connected-agent contexts. This path is shipped, not an assumed external user-written AGENTS.md. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Which agents and actions the ongoing project/organization authorizes, which approvals remain nondelegable and what spending or time rights every new agent task receives. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: In the one credited parent mode, org administrator sets binding server-wide rules and project administrator sets only narrower local rules; the policy setter rejects model agents outright. - Return-to-operation path: Stored administrator decision is combined into effective policy; new-task admission enforces the eligible agents and budget caps, and subsequent connected-agent task context receives policy rules and approval requirements. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Only a legitimate human admin has authority to change the continuing constitution; agents execute within its returned constraints. Discretionary governance remains at the parent recursion. - Evidence: policies, bridge context and rules, task admission. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high for hierarchy/admission/prompt return; lower for compliance with textual rules by an external model. - Caveats: Plain-language policy obedience depends on provider behavior; allowed agent lists and budget caps have stronger deterministic enforcement.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/670ef2c13fe29c9a7f414b48b2182b4727b90970/assessments/or/orco.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 670ef2c13fe29c9a7f414b48b2182b4727b90970\">670ef2c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T18:46:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/splashxmoon/friday\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Friday</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/splashxmoon/friday/tree/7db025c6513eae1c05a93f6e7ba58fd5ffad2a2a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7db025c6513eae1c05a93f6e7ba58fd5ffad2a2a\">7db025c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform an operator&#39;s work objective into code, tests, documents or other concrete repository artifacts and correct defects according to tool/source feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous requirements, codebase differences, unexpected test results and task-local implementation choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the assigned model-driven lane chooses substantive implementation/tool calls, interprets outcomes and revises its own artifact before reporting completion. - Decision owner: a Friday-managed coding-agent lane invoking its configured external Claude Code, Codex, other CLI or Friday Code model; not the UI, deterministic task allocator or model vendor&#39;s unrelated organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session state, local CLI adapter and worktree, role instructions, checkpoints, task board, test runner, manual permission mode and event history. - Closure path: human/lead task → owned lane executes substantive model/tool steps → workspace and check feedback → revised files/report → task complete or returned for rework. - Boundary reachability: README standard Code and Crew paths, src/runner/runner.ts run/turn interface, src/agent-mesh.ts assign/claim/finish and src/teammates.ts role handoff are shipped first-party integration paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-driven lane leaves only launch, log and gate code; that code cannot determine source-specific implementation by itself. - Evidence: pinned README, src/runner/runner.ts, src/agent-mesh.ts, src/teammates.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for owned role and executable paths, not for every vendor CLI combination&#39;s observed quality. - Caveats: provider CLI agents and models remain external execution dependencies; their internal agent hierarchies are not attributed to Friday.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference among simultaneously editing worker lanes by assigning file custody and returning conflict warnings to the agent before further edits. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two otherwise independent agent lanes claiming or editing the same file, incompatible interface changes and stale board/task ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: accept non-overlapping claimfiles paths; reject claims already owned by other lanes; on a conflicting diff, instruct the colliding worker to stop editing and consult or wait for the holder. - Decision owner: constructor-defined Friday mesh claim/ownership checks; subsequent peer negotiation is by task actors, not evidence of a dedicated autonomous S2 policy authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: files.held, files.released, board owns sets, heldBy, onDiff, agent messages and wake events. - Closure path: first lane holds path → second lane claims or changes it → Friday identifies holder and refuses claim or warns the violator → second lane must stop and coordinate or wait before continuing. - Boundary reachability: agent-visible claimfiles and releasefiles tools and automatic onDiff are part of shipped src/agent-mesh.ts integration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordination relation reaches worker behavior, but collision arbitration is a prewritten deterministic rule; peer dialogue alone does not prove an independent S2 agent making the binding priority policy. - Distinct S1 units: independent Friday-managed coding-agent lanes working in the same session/repository. - Inter-S1 disturbance: conflicting writes/claims to the same source path or a board-owned file. - Attenuating coordination relation: ownership claim, refused second claim and holder-specific warning on changed files. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: collision message explicitly directs the second worker to stop changing the file, ask the owner, or wait for release. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: it detects and returns a concrete cross-worker collision, rather than merely listing agents or assigning tasks. - Evidence: pinned src/agent-mesh.ts (holdFiles, releaseFiles, onDiff, heldBy) and README team-worktree instructions. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for the implemented narrow path. - Caveats: collision warning and cooperative file claiming are advisory; no proof that arbitrary external tools cannot violate the instructions or that the entire repository has transactional file locks.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: an authorized lead agent manages present board commitments, assignment, current blocked work, reallocation and completion under an operator&#39;s goal. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled team progress, uneven available capacity, failed checks/review, agents running out of usage, inconsistent task progress and unclear priorities across active lanes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the designated lead model can autonomously create/decompose a board, assign work, steer or stop a teammate in Crew mode, cancel/retry/reassign blocked tasks, inspect reports and decide the next current step. - Decision owner: the configured first-party-managed lead agent inside a Friday Code/Crew session; Friday enforces role permissions, and the human owner still has higher-level intervention rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: guarded posttasks, assigntask, managetask, steeragent; dependency/cycle checks, finite board, event projection, current budget/usage signals, deterministic dispatch, retries and lead reports. - Closure path: goal/current workload and progress evidence → lead model posts or adjusts the board/steers worker → Friday dispatches its authorized decision → reports/tests/usage/blocks return to lead → lead revises assignments or calls for remediation. - Boundary reachability: src/agent-mesh.ts offers board management to the lead and reserves steering to state.lead; src/team-selfrun.ts explicitly tells an enabled autonomous lead to choose the next valuable tasks, hand them out, decide open questions, and stop only at goal completion/budget/stall. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-work authority is not only deterministic queue management; an explicitly designated model-driven lead can choose and alter task partition and reprioritization through role-gated tools. This positive state requires the supported lead/Crew path, not passive solo-agent UI mode. - Whole-system current view: one session/room&#39;s board statuses, owners, checks, message reports, agent availability, usage limits and budget, bounded by the human&#39;s goal. - Current-control decision scope: board admission and redistribution, teammate redirection and when to try/stop/rework; not a demonstrated corporate-level resource or identity authority. - Evidence: pinned src/agent-mesh.ts (manageTask, postTasks, control, steer, round, reportBoard) and src/team-selfrun.ts; README Crew path. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for first-party-owned agent lead control mode. - Caveats: whether the chosen provider model actually exercises that discretion well is not observed; deterministic budgets and automated selection are C components within this A ownership claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: ask a separate model-driven reviewer to challenge a worker&#39;s own completion claim against its actual artifacts, task requirements and recorded proof, and return necessary corrections before board completion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unverified self-reported completion, missed requirements and defects not caught by the worker&#39;s own tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a different available reviewer lane reads another agent&#39;s task, report, proof and diff/source and returns a substantive approval or correction verdict. - Decision owner: the independently assigned model-driven reviewer lane, distinct from the task&#39;s implementing lane. Friday&#39;s check runner and verdict parser are support/gates rather than the substantive audit judge. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewOrPass, different-lane pickReviewer, awayDiff, proof/changes handoff, board.reviewed, bounded rework, configured tests, optional model-based goalRequest judge. - Closure path: worker reports finished task + actual diff/proof → Friday runs its checks → if eligible, separate reviewer sees task and evidence → reviewer decides pass or correction → failed verdict sends defects back to worker and repeats review, or terminal escalation after rework bound. - Boundary reachability: the standard multi-agent board review path src/agent-mesh.ts dispatches to a lane other than t.lane; onRunEnded records its verdict, and rework returns rejected findings. A separate task goal judge is available under src/goal.ts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in the supported second-review path, the reviewer model reads changed artifacts and makes a case-specific challenge that code cannot predefine. Automatic checks alone, a UI read-only panel, and an operator&#39;s final review are not counted as autonomous S3. - Claim being audited: a specific assigned board task is correctly finished and fits acceptance/requirements. - Ordinary reporting path: implementing lane&#39;s completion report, test commands and worktree changes. - Complementary access path: Friday presents the other reviewer source/worktree or the recorded diff and task proof, with independent reviewer instructions and test receipts rather than merely trusting the author&#39;s summary. - Independence boundary: different agent lane, not guaranteed different model vendor or fully isolated model context; source access depends on whether the reviewer runs on the same machine or receives a recorded diff. - Who acts on findings: mesh sends failed verdict and concrete instructions back to the original task lane for bounded repair; successful review unlocks dependent tasks. - Evidence: pinned src/agent-mesh.ts (runChecks, reviewOrPass, pickReviewer, round, boardRunEnded, rework, awayDiff), src/team-proof.ts, src/goal.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high in configured multi-agent second-review mode. - Caveats: Friday deliberately skips cross-review for qualifying small changes with real passing tests, no changed files or unavailable alternate reviewer; thus S3=A is conditional, not universal. Vendor/model or context independence is not proven beyond different lanes and evidence paths.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no demonstrated first-party decision loop that turns anticipated external developments into changes of the Friday work organization&#39;s future mission/capability envelope. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changed tool limits, learned model effectiveness, later repo errors and new scheduled tasks are sensed, but the cited response is route optimization or current execution within existing permissions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model/tool selection, replayed lessons and human-accepted reusable skills do not by themselves constitute durable first-party prospective-capability choice. - Decision owner: constructor-defined scoring rules and human skill approver for the observed adaptation mechanisms; no bounded autonomous Friday S4 organizational actor identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: route-learning feedback grades, model benchmark priors, routing.ts choice, Crew skill proposals and human acceptance, memory/automation setup. - Closure path: observed test/review feedback → model-fit estimate/current routing or proposed skill → later present task; no environmental forecast and S4 option evaluation leading to an authorized changed future organizational capability is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a feedback-driven heuristic and task-level learning are not enough to assign future-facing organization intelligence; even autonomous self-run leads act on the current user goal and board. - Evidence: pinned src/route-learning.ts, src/routing.ts, src/team-skills.ts, src/team-selfrun.ts, README memory/automation sections. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: this is not a denial of ordinary learning, future-oriented user projects, or the possibility that a parent organization uses Friday as its S4 tool.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no supported ultimate-identity or constitutional-policy adjudication loop for a Friday installation was found. - Disturbance / variety regulated: access modes, permissions, publishing authority, spending/usage bounds and goal text arrive as user-set constraints or constructor safeguards rather than deliberated organization policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the person establishes the goal, access level, budgets, and final commit/PR/deployment approval; model-driven workers cannot legitimize a change of identity or waive these user boundaries. - Decision owner: external local user/owner and platform permission constraints; no first-party S5 policy-maker with a demonstrated return to the running organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Crew read/ask/full permissions, src/team-budget.ts, approval events, self-run prohibitions on default-branch push/deployment/data deletion/spending, manual final review. - Closure path: human chooses constraints → Friday enforces them during tasks → agent operates in the bounded space; no question of ultimate mission/identity is assessed by an S5 authority and promulgated as a legitimate new policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an autonomous lead controlling work is S3, not by itself a constitutional S5, and static access/approval settings are control tools rather than a policy arbitration loop. - Evidence: pinned README human review/Crew settings, src/teammates.ts, src/team-selfrun.ts, src/team-budget.ts, src/runner/runner.ts. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a human operator or organization may exercise S5 outside the shipped runtime; such parent authority is not silently imported.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d1c43df574e84c7b5d9a3e8b68ab4ea0be83d739/assessments/fr/friday.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d1c43df574e84c7b5d9a3e8b68ab4ea0be83d739\">d1c43df &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T18:26:34+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/one-king-studio/hildi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hildi</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/one-king-studio/hildi/tree/d5fb376ab918e02facdbdcc0fbf00dabb3f6a806\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d5fb376ab918e02facdbdcc0fbf00dabb3f6a806\">d5fb376 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-led coding and file/system task execution through observed tool feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User instructions, unknown file state, command errors, provider tool-call responses and subtask results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select tools/arguments, decide edits and shell commands, consult source, request child work and revise steps based on returned observations. - Decision owner: The model in each supported session/child agent loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TAgentLoop, OpenAI-compatible model transport, approval gate, command classification, SQLite session store, socket runtime and bounded tools. - Closure path: User/ambient context → LLM tool selection → execution under approval → tool result returned in conversation → subsequent model turn or final answer; child completions may wake the parent for another turn. - Boundary reachability: The shipped hildi-agent runtime constructs THarnessSession with TAgentLoop and tool registry; the TUI drives the same socket session and child processes run the same agent mechanism. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic approval/security and session persistence remain without a model, but they cannot choose which code changes solve the current task. - Evidence: HildiAgent, HildiHarnessSession tool wiring, HildiSessionRuntime. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high in wired agent/tool loop; provider efficacy not exercised. - Caveats: Headless model inference requires a configured external provider; source-level claims are not live model task benchmarks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Suppress duplicate work and stale-state oscillation among independent model-operated sessions in the same workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two peers simultaneously starting the same task, overwriting one another&#39;s workspace changes or acting on stale task/board state. - Distinct S1 units: Separate independently started live agent sessions detected via sessionruntimes, explicitly distinguished from parent-owned spawned child sessions. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Competing peers race to claim an open card; without the guarded claim both could work it and issue conflicting changes. Protocol also identifies path ownership/commit cadence as conflict risks. - Attenuating coordination relation: Shared board decisions plus taskclaim&#39;s atomic compare-and-swap, holder/age refusal and mandatory claim-before-change rule; peer mode is selected from actual live runtime presence rather than a static multi-agent flag. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Losing claim returns holder/status and how long held to the requesting model; board notices and mode-transition ambient messages prompt a fresh boardread, revised claim/task choice or peer protocol. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Conflict arises between distinct independent S1 agents; a first-party prevention gate identifies its winner/loser, and the model-directed protocol uses that feedback to adjust later operations. Spawned children are deliberately treated as delegation, not peer negotiation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a different card, defer modifications, negotiate path ownership/commit protocol and record a new shared decision when contention or changing peer presence is observed. - Decision owner: Each model-driven peer agent, with deterministic SQLite CAS as enforcement, not as discretionary coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: HildiCoordination live-peer query and prompt, TSqliteTaskStore.Claim, TTaskTool.ClaimCard, boardpost/read, task update and stale-source warnings. - Closure path: Contending work intentions → atomic claim/board visibility → successful claim or explicit holder refusal → returned tool/ambient feedback → agent revises own next task and writes a shared coordination decision when needed. - Boundary reachability: A stored workspace session wires TTaskTool and TBoardTools directly into the model registry; DetectCoordinationMode and NoticeBoard run in the supported per-turn session path with peer joining/leaving messages. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The CAS cannot decide which alternative task, path ownership or coordination protocol to adopt after conflict; the model session owns that discretion. - Evidence: HildiCoordination, ClaimCard, atomic Claim, NoticeBoard and tool registration. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for peer-level claim contention; not evidence of an always-successful multi-agent planning consensus. - Caveats: Claim-before-change is an instruction, not a forced path-level reservation for every write; external tools may bypass it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Maintain and intervene in current child-agent commitments in a parent-session work portfolio. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Active child availability, asynchronous failures, task ownership changes, approval stalls and urgent operator takeover during live work. - Whole-system current view: The model obtains child status/slot acknowledgments and ambient terminal results, while the human operator&#39;s sidebar/spy shows the full current child roster and direct child transcript/live stream in the same parent composition. - Current-control decision scope: Model chooses delegate/message/abort/release actions, task scope, child follow-ups and parent next work; human chooses abort/release/promote of a specific child with live roster context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In the base mode, the parent model allocates/reallocates work and responds to child outcomes; in the supervised mode, the operator decides whether a live child remains under the parent&#39;s current control. - Decision owner: Base A mode — parent model; parent P mode — legitimate operator acting through the TUI for that task composition. SubagentSupervisor only enforces capacities, socket commands, event relay and lineage. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Child process supervisor, max/depth caps, message/abort/release command handlers, parent prompt queue, child roster, observer socket, status board and session lineage detachment. - Closure path: Base: delegation → independent child run → result/aborted/gone event → ambient parent wake → model changes next commitments. Parent mode: operator inspects roster/live child → aborts/releases/promotes → runtime command changes child operation/lineage, and takeover coordination notice reaches the former parent. - Boundary reachability: HildiSessionRuntime creates TSubagentSupervisor and registers the model-callable child tools; TUI ShowSpy, OnSpyAction and AnnounceTakeover are shipped with the sibling client and address the live parent/child session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The base choice of which task to delegate or revise is model discretionary; the optional human takeover is an independently evidenced alternative decisive owner. Registry limits and event loops enforce but do not select either owner&#39;s intervention. - Evidence: subagent tools and service, supervisor, runtime ambient return, TUI takeover path. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for delegated current-control and observed parent mode, bounded to one composition. - Caveats: This is not a whole-organization manager of all unrelated peer sessions. The P mode is optional and its board notification is best-effort; loss of the notification can weaken parent awareness although lineage detachment still changes what the parent can command.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Supply an independent-from-summary observation path into a delegated child&#39;s actual conversation/tool operations for potential corrective audit by an operator or composed reviewer. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parent receives only a summarized child result, which may hide failed tool actions or unexpectedly broad work; a distinct observer can inspect recorded tool/action reality. - Claim being audited: Whether a child actually performed the stated actions and whether its work should continue under parent authority. - Ordinary reporting path: TSubagentSupervisor sends summarized completion/status as an ambient result to the parent model. - Complementary access path: Separate observe-only TSessionClient socket to the child&#39;s runtime plus a stored per-child transcript tail, showing raw tool, error, assistant and live event stream. - Independence boundary: The observer is not the child&#39;s own summary and does not gain a drive role; it reads first-party child runtime/store evidence directly. It is not independent of the physical host&#39;s persistence or evidence integrity, and does not itself make an audit verdict. - Who acts on findings: In the shipped UI a human can inspect and abort/promote/release a child, with takeover notice and detachment; a composed independent audit judgment/mandate remains required for autonomous S3 ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Compare direct child execution evidence with reported status and decide whether/how to return corrective intervention; the specific observation/intervention seam exists, but no dedicated independent autonomous reviewer is wired. - Decision owner: Potential human auditor/constructor consumer; no shipped agent-owned S3 judgment. The TUI supplies direct access and intervention construction points, not an automatic auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only session observer, historical message tail, live stream renderer, child status, abort/release/promote controls and board takeover status. - Closure path: Parent result claim → optional direct child spy and transcript examination → operator may intervene via runtime command and parent board notice; an autonomous audit actor and systematic checking policy would require composition. - Boundary reachability: Sidebar/ShowSpy and TSubagentSpy.Open are shipped UI paths; OnSpyAction calls the supported runtime command and AnnounceTakeover publishes a corrective status for the former parent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A second direct observer and corrective control surfaces are not just the ordinary result summary, but no first-party model auditor decides independent findings; accordingly the scoped S3-specific constructor is C, not A. - Evidence: HildiHarnessSubagentSpy, TUI ShowSpy / OnSpyAction, runtime ordinary child reporting. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; the claim is deliberately limited to complementary observation and possible corrective return, not guaranteed independent audit. - Caveats: The user&#39;s decision may be ordinary supervision rather than a formal audit; an observed and acted-on discrepancy is not demonstrated by a live trace in this review.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No separately closed process that evaluates external future possibilities and adapts the workspace organization or operating capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing providers, project context and tool availability may require new skills or working strategy, but the installed agent mostly responds within the current task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge external prospective capability needs and return a strategic adaptation into present operation; no such closed first-party decision owner is installed. - Decision owner: User/developer selects skills, MCP providers and runtime upgrades; a model chooses on-demand current tools, board entries and task plans. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MCP tool reconciliation, dynamic prompt assembly, skill loading, checkpoint/handoff, workspace board, goals and current task queue. - Closure path: Tools/skills can change with configuration and context checkpoints can carry a recent summary; none supplies a future-oriented outside assessment and authoritative capability-adaptation decision returned to S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A static skill or saved plan may shape next work, but neither is a prospective adaptation regulator with continuing responsibility. - Evidence: turn assembly, skills, MCP provider, feature statuses. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded shipped modes. - Caveats: An external agent might use Hildi to maintain/evolve a project, but such an additional organization&#39;s strategic loop is not Hildi&#39;s standard runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No complete runtime constitutional-identity or ultimate-policy adjudication and return loop at the assessed agent organization level. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Risky shell actions, approval scopes, task-card moderation and peer protocol changes remain local work/coordination decisions rather than identity-level constitutional conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Authoritatively resolve a conflict about organizational identity or ultimate purpose, then return a binding policy decision to all affected ongoing operation; not present. - Decision owner: Operator grants tool permissions and moderates shared records; model agents negotiate path/task protocols; maintainers decide repository policy outside the shipped runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Command effect classification and approval, shared board moderation, operator task reassignment and peer coordination prompt. - Closure path: User approval allows/denies tools and peer board decisions guide later tasks, but no identity-level matter → legitimate ultimate-policy authority → binding return to the live organization is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Claim ownership, operator takeover and shared protocol negotiation affect present work allocation (S2/S3), not the durable ultimate purpose or identity of the system. - Evidence: approval, board tools, task moderation, coordination protocol. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded absence. - Caveats: The public project&#39;s actual maintainer identity/governance decisions are adjacent, not a governing organ reached through the live harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/63dd717d3de738410edbc0ef7a65853126bb511c/assessments/hi/hildi.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 63dd717d3de738410edbc0ef7a65853126bb511c\">63dd717 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T18:44:01+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kushagrathisside/bridle\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bridle</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kushagrathisside/bridle/tree/9b9b9f868c18958a921b6967fcd7d64acde877fc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9b9b9f868c18958a921b6967fcd7d64acde877fc\">9b9b9f8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Iterative model-led code edits, repository investigation and verification for a user or board issue. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown project state, errors, failed checks, insufficient implementation and user-requested changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the next file/tool call, edit and follow-up based on returned evidence. - Decision owner: Chat model or the currently active role-specific member model session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent::runturn, tools server, file/command/Git tools, tool policy, llama.cpp provider, turn bounds and per-member context. - Closure path: Prompt or issue → model decides tool call → policy checks → execution result returns → model revises code or completes/updates board. - Boundary reachability: Agent::formember constructs the working model/tool agent; desktop runissue supplies real member tools, issue brief and chat job. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static policy and persistence cannot select which code change actually solves the task. - Evidence: agent loop, member-run wiring. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for wired model/tool loop. - Caveats: Windows-only deployment verified by author on one machine; no live provider task run reproduced in this review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Avoid competing project issue claims and overlapping shared-workspace member runs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Duplicate issue creation, duplicate claim/retry and conflicting same-project file mutations by distinct members. - Distinct S1 units: Independently prompted PM, engineering and QA model turns, serial on one provider rather than simultaneous models. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Two members can try to claim the same card or re-create a task, or act on shared files with unfinished earlier edits. - Attenuating coordination relation: BoardStore atomic claim/status gate, stable issue-key deduplication, dependency/ready checks and one project RunGuard lock. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: boardcreate reports Already exists with the issue id; invalid claims report existing status/assignee; subsequent models read changed ready/blocked board and comments. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The checks prevent concrete duplicate ownership and mutually interfering access, returning a conflict-sensitive outcome to affected agent turns. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Determine claim winner/reject duplicate or exclusive project-run admission. No model-owned adaptive S2 conflict policy is shipped. - Decision owner: Deterministic first-party BoardStore/RunGuard constructor path and fixed operator/developer rules; models choose issues but do not own the exclusion algorithm. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Exclusive board locks, claim preconditions, issue-key collision checks, dependency gates, run lock and Git checkpoints. - Closure path: Repeated/competing issue or member-run attempt → duplicate/claim/lock verdict → member receives error/current board state → only eligible next work proceeds. - Boundary reachability: Model-callable boardcreate/claim are injected into actual member agent; desktop runissue takes project lock and board claim before starting a model turn. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The same coordination gates operate without model discretion; technical-manager advice to avoid touching the same files is not enforced file leasing. - Evidence: board implementation, board tools, run recovery/lock. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for this bounded S2 constructor. - Caveats: No fine-grained path leases, parallel worktree model agents, global conflict negotiation or dynamic model ownership of the lock rule.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Manage the current multi-member issue portfolio: resources, priorities, dependencies, review and interventions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Wrong owner, failed task, blocked review, unbalanced workload or stalled work chain. - Whole-system current view: boardlist/show expose all issue statuses, priorities, owners, blockers and comments to lead model; desktop board shows all current work and member load to human. - Current-control decision scope: Lead model can create multi-step plans, choose assignments, dependencies and priorities, reassign existing issues or change status; human can assign, hold, return, rerun or stop work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model lead chooses/revises active team commitments; in optional parent mode human directly changes current assignments/states or interrupts a run. - Decision owner: Base A — role-scoped lead model. Parent P — human desktop operator. Board and runner enforce each decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: boardplan, lead boardupdate reassignment/status, board overview, ready priority queue, dependency solver and operator actions. - Closure path: Base: model surveys board → creates/updates cross-member commitments → store records choices → next members see new ready/blocked assignments. Parent: human inspects board/runner → assigns, moves, holds or stops → upcoming turns obey updated board. - Boundary reachability: The shipped member runner injects board tools with lead privileges; desktop board commands edit the same BoardStore and control active turns. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The base plan and follow-up status choices are discretionary model decisions, whereas the UI is a separately evidenced human control mode; the ready queue alone is not an owner. - Evidence: lead tool permissions and plan, member runner and UI changes, guide. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for reachable rights, not model decision success rates. - Caveats: Team model roles are simulated with one model, in serial, and upstream says PM outcomes are weakly measured.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently check an engineer&#39;s reported completion against repository source and test reality through a separate QA role. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A worker&#39;s done/inreview claim can hide incomplete, invalid or unverified changes. - Claim being audited: Engineering issue completion according to acceptance criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: Engineer&#39;s boardcomment, status and answer, plus runner-generated changed-files note. - Complementary access path: QA member directly reads actual files, Git diffs and runs project checks with standard tools, beyond the worker&#39;s own report. - Independence boundary: QA is another prompted member with its own model conversation and tools; model weights, repository and policy are shared, not independent institutional evidence. - Who acts on findings: QA lead/tester model can flag failed acceptance, send an inreview issue to ready or create a corrective bug issue; a lead may close reviewed work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: QA agent makes its own evidence-based accept/reject judgment and changes review status or follow-up obligations. - Decision owner: The model acting in the separately instantiated QA member, not the worker whose claims are under test. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: QA role prompts, independent member session, read/test/Git tools, QA boardupdate rights and acceptance/dependency fields. - Closure path: Engineer reports work → QA issue ready or original card in review → separate QA model reads raw files/tests → judges results → corrective boardupdate/rework affects future engineering and S3 choices. - Boundary reachability: Shipped roster includes QA lead/tester; PM can create a dependent QA task, and desktop runissue initializes the QA member with file/test and board tools. QA status rights explicitly allow review return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A QA model chooses whether observed source satisfies criteria and what corrective card/status to emit; test commands only supply evidence. - Evidence: QA personas, QA review rights, separate member execution. - Basis: structural + documented team review scenario. - Confidence: medium; no live adversarial QA finding reproduced. - Caveats: QA is not mandatory for each work item, and accepting a worker&#39;s text without checking raw evidence would not exercise S3. This is logical role separation, not an external auditor.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No independently closed external/prospective option-development and capability-return loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New tools/models, evolving dependencies and changing external requirements might necessitate adaptation; existing roles handle assigned work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Evaluate future environmental options and implement a changed present capability; no such model decision/return pipeline is wired. - Decision owner: User manually changes models, web settings and project design; model researcher responds to current tickets. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Research team prompt, optional web tools, multiple manually chosen model slots, ideas queue and unbuilt fleet roadmap. - Closure path: Research can produce a ticket answer or an idea another ticket may use, but no standard future-oriented strategic loop governs current operating structure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Research role, temporal storage and roadmap features are not proof of prospective organizational adaptation. - Evidence: roles, team limits, README status. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high within shipped mode. - Caveats: A human can request a future-planning issue, but such task compliance would not establish the ongoing metasystem function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime identity-level or ultimate-policy dilemma resolution loop with authorized decision return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Secret access, risky tools, member activation, role assignments and approval/security boundaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Settle contested system mission or ultimate policy and enact that verdict for later operations; not installed. - Decision owner: Human edits staffing/settings; code enforces pre-authored role and security limits; model members decide current tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolPolicy, TeamStore in machine config, roster validation, role rights and human crew editor. - Closure path: Operator changes roster/headcount or policy configuration and subsequent tasks use it; there is no separately evidenced identity-policy proposal, human constitutional judgment and returned governing choice. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A safety gate, role prompt and staffing UI bound routine tasks but do not give a model or a human a first-party runtime constitutional S5 loop. - Evidence: roster and role structure, roster editor, security rules. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high at the project-team boundary. - Caveats: The user&#39;s legitimate project-governance rights and upstream maintainer governance exist outside this narrowly observed deployed mechanism.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0c63110cef0742c79d16ab5eaaa6dac7be449479/assessments/ku/kushagrathisside-bridle.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0c63110cef0742c79d16ab5eaaa6dac7be449479\">0c63110 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T16:47:33+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/BubbleBuffer/BubblePaw-Core\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BubblePaw Core</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/BubbleBuffer/BubblePaw-Core/tree/2f98827beda9f2c988368c99ee9fa69120899dff\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2f98827beda9f2c988368c99ee9fa69120899dff\">2f98827 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform workspace tasks through durable model/tool sessions and reusable child work actors. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain task progress, tool errors, context limits, interrupted external effects and child questions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose next action, tool, child task or final answer from the current observations. - Decision owner: The model actor driven by the native Harness model/tool loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SQLite history/effects, ToolDispatcher, CodeMode/workspace tools, provider adapters, approvals, queued steering and explicit resume. - Closure path: Prepared model request → model-selected tools → durable tool results → subsequent model request; child completion can wake the parent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Own runtime.rs executes the authoritative tool loop. The API provider witnesses inference-only transport; external Codex/Claude adapters do not donate their inner control functions. - Evidence: crates/bubblepaw-core/src/runtime.rs; crates/bubblepaw-core/src/dispatch.rs; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/agents.rs; docs/providers.md; docs/child-sessions.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed. - Boundary reachability: The normal bubblepaw runner wires Harness, tools and model providers; API provider mode and registered child profiles reach the same first-party loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate shared-workspace write interference between autonomous workspace child sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two workspace-profile children otherwise inherit the same filesystem and can overwrite one another; descendants may accidentally use the wrong root workspace. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose isolated child workspaces for concurrent writers and use returned branch/workspace state when continuing them. - Decision owner: The parent model actor selects worktree:true through the shipped tools.agent interface; git and workspace resolvers enforce that choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-child git worktree/branch, nearest-isolated-ancestor workspace resolution, scope-bound file/exec tools, status receipts and safe retention/cleanup. - Closure path: Parent selects isolation → child runs against its effective checkout → spawn/status returns workspace/branch/worktree facts → parent continues/steers the corresponding session without sharing its write surface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The credited decision is the agent-selectable conflict-attenuation mode, not deterministic git or session plurality. It is available to the running parent without custom host coordination code. - Evidence: docs/child-worktrees.md; docs/child-sessions.md; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/agents.rs; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/worktrees.rs. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Optional mode; default workspaces are shared, and isolation does not automatically merge conflicting results or prove semantic conflict resolution.. - Boundary reachability: Registered model-facing workspace profiles expose the worktree field; agents.rs creates the binding at admission and subsequent child tools resolve that same workspace.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate current commitments and compute across a parent task and its descendant work tree. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stale/running/failed/waiting children, finite shared tool allowance/deadline and unfinished commitments competing for that allowance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect descendant status, cancel or steer child commitments, and assign fresh per-child tool/deadline bounds for the next turn. - Decision owner: The parent model actor owns these current choices within host-imposed maximums. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Descendant-tree status with unread/results, stale-child reminders, child completion wake, inherited budget/deadline clamping and subtree cancellation cleanup. - Closure path: State/reminder → parent observes current descendant tree/result → parent cancels, redirects or issues a bounded next turn → child runtime applies the changed commitment/allowance and returns status/results. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Credit is bounded to the parent task organization: the actor sees and can change ongoing child work on behalf of that whole. Host quota waiting and concurrency limits merely enforce ceilings; they do not own the parent decision. - Evidence: docs/child-sessions.md; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/agents.rs; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/agenthandles.rs; crates/bubblepaw-core/src/runtime.rs. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Spawn/decomposition alone is not the witness. Controls cannot enlarge an active turn by steering; fresh limits apply on later child turns and remain clamped by originating bounds. No separate parent-human mode is inferred from approval UI.. - Boundary reachability: The ordinary runner model tool set includes agentstatus, agentsend and cancel with the originating parent ownership checks; no custom supervisor is needed for these decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No complementary independent operational-audit loop is supplied in the normal workspace runner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The possible organizational disturbance associated with the inspected mechanisms. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive organizational right established in this supported mode. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner established; developer/operator constraints remain external decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Context-recall probes/judges, replay/chapter-bench commands, direct child transcript access, logs, structured-output validation and ordinary tool checks. - Closure path: No corresponding organizational return loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Probe scoring belongs to explicit replay/evaluation rather than a normal-session corrective controller. Generic research/child tools can be composed into review, but no specifically wired independent audit judgment plus corrective return is shipped for normal workspace operations. Source-level verification is adjacent. - Evidence: crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/probes.rs; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/main.rs; crates/bubblepaw-core/src/observation.rs; docs/verification.md; docs/child-sessions.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No outside-and-then organizational adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The possible organizational disturbance associated with the inspected mechanisms. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive organizational right established in this supported mode. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner established; developer/operator constraints remain external decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Summary/chapter decision models, compaction/recall, selectable model roles, live provider configuration and research children. - Closure path: No corresponding organizational return loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Context organization and provider reconfiguration preserve current operation. Research and recall mechanisms do not by themselves investigate prospective environmental change and return selected capability adaptations. Replay model comparisons are evaluation, not a deployed adaptation authority. - Evidence: docs/decision-providers.md; docs/live-config.md; docs/context-compaction.md; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/agents.rs; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/probes.rs. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity/ultimate-policy arbitration loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The possible organizational disturbance associated with the inspected mechanisms. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive organizational right established in this supported mode. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner established; developer/operator constraints remain external decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Ask/Allow/Deny/Auto approvals, model-based bounded auto checks, profile narrowing, live config updates and inherited child authority. - Closure path: No corresponding organizational return loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The returned decisions concern ordinary tool permissions and execution constraints, not an identity/ultimate-policy matter at this recursion. No first-party escalation and authoritative arbitration/return path for that function is packaged. - Evidence: docs/approvals.md; docs/live-config.md; crates/bubblepaw-core/src/approvals/mod.rs; crates/bubblepaw-core/src/approvals/modes.rs; docs/child-sessions.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9ff383b7ec14a8c25bdb4ee0ad185837493720aa/assessments/bu/bubblepaw-core.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9ff383b7ec14a8c25bdb4ee0ad185837493720aa\">9ff383b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:34:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/NeoPanic/harness3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Harness³</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/NeoPanic/harness3/tree/cb6c8e57eb0adac3f2ec1f4da1a98e52e2967063\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision cb6c8e57eb0adac3f2ec1f4da1a98e52e2967063\">cb6c8e5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute bounded user-published work through model-selected tools, report results, and optionally decide to split tasks for peer execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variable tasks, tool results, failed actions and results from completed children. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an instantiated Agent chooses next model/tool action, whether to use the board claim/split tools, and its work contribution. - Decision owner: model-driven independent Agent within the shipped Harness run; platform validates tool and task authority but does not choose the Agent&#39;s tool sequence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Preset bindings, runtime policy, receipts/checkpoints, SQLite Run state, tool-safety limits and claim prerequisite. - Closure path: executed tool output feeds subsequent model reasoning; settled Run and child results feed board continuation or user-visible contribution. - Boundary reachability: worker runner instantiates the shipping Harness with each Preset; normal board and Goal requests reach this path, independently of demo/test code. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model has substantive task execution/splitting discretion; deterministic runtime and human task publisher set only operating constraints. - Evidence: crates/harness3-core/src/engine.rs#L65-L185; crates/harness3-worker/src/runner.rs#L55-L135; crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/board/mod.rs#L277-L325. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: high for source path; no provider-backed E2E performed. - Caveats: external tools may be unavailable and effects require idempotency beyond checkpointing; Demo output is not real research evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: resolve contention for the same operational work item and stabilize parent-child availability as peers execute and split work. - Distinct S1 units: separately provisioned model/tool Agent instances, each bound to a Run, Preset snapshot and independent local state. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing peers could both treat the same pending task as theirs; child and parent attempts could race while a predecessor is unresolved, causing duplicate work or invalid integration. - Attenuating coordination relation: atomic claim checks current task state and Preset compatibility, rejects a second claimant; staged children only become runnable after a successful source Run, while parent waits until all children finish; failed children block the parent. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a rejected claim returns a conflict to the Agent, pending/claimed state changes what may be read/claimed next, and completed children reoffer the parent for a new integrating Agent. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the specific multiple-owner race and dependent-work collision are regulated through conditional transactional ownership and state transitions, not just message delivery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: peer contention and out-of-order execution of shared task dependencies. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the first-party board contract exposes task claim/split/feedback to peers, but collision arbitration is deterministic; no autonomous regulator decides among competing peer priorities. - Decision owner: deterministic SQLite transaction for conflict arbitration; individual S1 Agents independently choose which compatible task to attempt. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shared board, generation/Run identity checks, atomic UPDATE, dependency state, event log and bounded task count. - Closure path: successful claim gives one execution owner; unsuccessful claimant receives 409 and can choose differently; parent is made pending after children complete or blocked on failure. - Boundary reachability: board tools are automatically injected into regular task-board Agent runs, not a theoretical adapter; claim enforcement occurs before work tools are usable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: coordination function and feedback path are real, but an autonomous S2 decision actor with cross-peer conflict discretion is not provided. The S2-specific constructor/claim primitive warrants C, not A. - Evidence: crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/board/mod.rs#L277-L325; crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/board/mod.rs#L333-L383; crates/harness3-worker/src/runner.rs#L18-L47; docs/task-board.zh-CN.md#L1-L39. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this is narrow coordination of task ownership/dependencies, not general negotiation of conflicting goals, cost allocation, global fairness or distributed consensus.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current operating capability/instance allocation against admitted work and shared resource limits, with distinct autonomous routing and human fleet-control modes. - Whole-system current view: pending board tasks, executable Preset catalog and model availability, active worker slots / reserved instances; in human fleet mode, reported capacity, fleet status and outstanding operations. - Current-control decision scope: choose which registered capabilities to provision for currently pending work; in parent mode change desired instance counts, scale or retire fleets under the current global quota. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uneven present task demand, incompatible/missing Presets, model availability, instance/worker scarcity and fleet size drift. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model router selects a Preset name/version/reason from the live eligible catalog for each actionable task; separately an authorized human chooses fleet target/termination. - Decision owner: autonomous bounded routing model in the base mode; legitimate operator in the distinct fleet mode. Deterministic Rust/SQLite performs candidate validation, quota, retry and reconciliation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: catalog filter, route timeout/backoff, global worker/instance limits, fleet revision checks, operation receipts, reconcile loop. - Closure path: accepted router selection is provisioned into a real instance and Run; failed/no-capacity decisions defer task for another attempt. In parent mode operator target writes desired state and fleet reconciler creates/drains actual instances, with operation/status returned. - Boundary reachability: model-backed router is wired into normal worker loop when a real provider is configured; fleet scale/terminate commands and the reconciliation loop are standard local API/worker capabilities, not a roadmap. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the base model makes a nontrivial capability-selection decision within live constraints, whereas Rust only validates and provisions. Human fleet mode owns a different concrete current-resource choice; this does not make all enforcement an autonomous S3 policy. - Evidence: crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/routing.rs#L91-L235; crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/routing.rs#L264-L315; crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/board/mod.rs#L243-L275; crates/harness3-platform/src/control/fleets.rs#L76-L137; crates/harness3-platform/src/control/fleets.rs#L202-L270; crates/harness3-worker/src/scheduler.rs#L20-L60. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: medium-high for real-model routing and fleet closure; no paid-model E2E. - Caveats: the router is a deliberately bounded single-call classifier, not an unlimited supervisor; its discretion is narrow current Preset choice. Demo uses deterministic selection. The human mode manages fleets and does not grant a human co-ownership of the same automatic board route in one run.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently inspect frozen operational contributions against specified checks, produce an evidence-linked judgment and drive bounded repair/acceptance gating. - Claim being audited: Goal work artifacts and final contributions support the specified acceptance/evidence checks. - Ordinary reporting path: operational S1 Runs and their artifacts/Goal contributions, including model-generated completion claims. - Complementary access path: a freshly allocated verification instance receives a frozen artifact manifest and separate paginated reader that checks hashes, read coverage and version; it does not inherit worker tools or writable workspace. - Independence boundary: new instance/session and reviewer-only evidence/report tools; different scoped role from original worker, even though both might use an LLM provider and the same operator config. - Who acts on findings: platform deterministically applies passed/failed/unknown/stale verdict states and, if preauthorized, launches bounded affected-task rework; human remains final Goal acceptor. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported claims, stale shared context, missing/changed artifact evidence, quality defects and false success on incomplete Runs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independent model reviewer judges each required check as passed/failed/unknown with reasons and evidence references, separate from the originating worker. - Decision owner: fresh autonomous reviewer Agent owns the audit judgment; deterministic coverage/immutability gates and rework rules enforce/act on that verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: frozen hashes, per-artifact complete-read records, no plugin inheritance, bounded review timeout, manifest revision, immutable verdict, automatic rework quota and human acceptance. - Closure path: accepted verdict after successful reviewer Run becomes passed/changes-requested/inconclusive; failed findings can rerun related tasks and re-verify, while passed status gates later human acceptance. Stale evidence blocks acceptance. - Boundary reachability: optional verifier policy is supported on normal Goal and research workflows, and the worker runner launches its real configured Preset/model with restricted reviewer tools; it is not merely CI or Demo. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: semantic audit judgment comes from an independent Agent, not from the source worker or deterministic parser. Enforcement of complete reads does not magically prove external truth. - Evidence: crates/harness3-platform/src/collaboration/verification/mod.rs#L120-L263; crates/harness3-platform/src/collaboration/verification/mod.rs#L343-L616; crates/harness3-worker/src/runner.rs#L95-L135; docs/verification.zh-CN.md#L1-L42. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: high for shipped independent channel; medium for actual review quality without E2E. - Caveats: checks are chosen by the user; reads demonstrate manifest coverage and integrity, not independent verification of external facts. The shared task board alone does not activate this Goal verifier.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party externally sensing prospective adaptation decision loop changing the platform&#39;s future capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: prospective shifts in task mix, technology, model efficacy or external environment not shown as managed future threats/opportunities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no agent-owned search/option/adoption loop with external future signal and return into Preset repertoire or system strategy. - Decision owner: not established; operator can publish new Presets, and individual S1s can do user-requested research. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user-directed research template, Preset versioning, task router, shared context, verification/rework and plugin catalogue. - Closure path: existing Preset selection and repair react to present work; no demonstrated future distinction yields adopted capability change through an S4 route. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning, multi-agent research and selecting among already available Presets are not automatically S4. - Evidence: crates/harness3-platform/src/collaboration/research/mod.rs#L1-L130; crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/routing.rs#L91-L177; docs/managed-agents.zh-CN.md#L1-L115. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an operator could build an adaptive Preset pipeline but no in-boundary autonomous or parent-governed S4 closure is evidenced. ### Absence scope\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime ultimate identity/constitutional arbitration loop beyond static, operator-authored Preset and platform policies. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potentially conflicting organizational values or who the collective is; no first-party escalation of such a dispute is traced. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no exposed agent or legitimate parent path from a raised identity-policy issue through authoritative judgment back into next operation. - Decision owner: operator authors immutable Presets and config; these are static parent-set constraints rather than an evidenced ongoing S5 loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: versioned Presets, tool binding/security checks, human Goal acceptance, model router restrictions and cancellation. - Closure path: ordinary task approval/review and preset publication changes normal capabilities, not established policy conflict resolution after a surfaced issue. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: authorizing a tool, passing an acceptance check or selecting a task specialist does not decide constitutional identity or ultimate policy. - Evidence: crates/harness3-core/src/policy.rs#L1-L22; crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/routing.rs#L137-L177; crates/harness3-platform/src/collaboration/coordination.rs#L135-L175; docs/managed-agents.zh-CN.md#L1-L115. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the human operator does set top-level deployment constraints, but no function-specific proposed/contested ultimate-policy closure is demonstrated merely by being able to configure them. ### Absence scope\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2d0bcd786318bfe5e21d11d3c07a020adb044bba/assessments/ne/neopanic-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2d0bcd786318bfe5e21d11d3c07a020adb044bba\">2d0bcd7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T19:12:59+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Gavriel-lab/luma-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Luma Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Gavriel-lab/luma-harness/tree/24dbf80a75a4bce048739bd2f149c5d447569b77\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 24dbf80a75a4bce048739bd2f149c5d447569b77\">24dbf80 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform a scoped work ticket&#39;s substantive file edits, project commands, test checks and deliverable/report production under the approved human workspace and acceptance criteria. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous implementation details, variable source files, compilation/test failures, path-specific edits and unexpected allowed tool outputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide concrete code/text modifications, permitted read/write/edit/run tool sequence, retry behavior and honest report of tests, artifacts and remaining unknowns. - Decision owner: the registered first-party-managed Pi worker model in PiRunner and runPiBridge, backed by the configured member&#39;s external model API. Luma controls the invocation/permission boundary; the unwrapped external Pi SDK is not credited with unrelated higher functions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-member API Profile, host model forwarder, authorized ContextPacket, short-lived Broker token, validateControlledPath, restricted tools and project command runner, event ordering, heartbeat/attempt lineage and staged delivery. - Closure path: approved task/dispatch → Pi worker claims unique attempt and obtains current context → model chooses allowed changes/commands → workspace/test feedback → submitted artifact paths/checks/unknowns → rejection for repair or controlled acceptance. - Boundary reachability: apps/desktop/connectors/pi/worker.mjs, runner.mjs and controlled-tools.mjs are shipped local executor paths and connected to current Broker authority. docs/PI.md explicitly distinguishes real read/write/test from API-only chat. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model-driven execution loop, Broker/permissions can accept only submissions and enforce scope, not choose task-specific edits or error corrections. - Evidence: pinned docs/PI.md, apps/desktop/connectors/pi/worker.mjs, runner.mjs, controlled-tools.mjs, bridgeBroker.cjs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for embedded Pi mode and first-party wiring, bounded by controlled fixture rather than live model account E2E. - Caveats: Pi project processes run with the host OS user&#39;s privileges, not an OS sandbox; real provider entitlements and other CLI versions remain independently qualified.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct implemented lateral conflict attenuation between independent worker operations with returned peer-behavior feedback is sufficiently established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple workers and dependencies coexist, and a second attempt can be stale, but no demonstrated runtime resolution of two independent worker edits/claims against the same contested file/resource is specified. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Broker deduplicates requests, invalidates stale attempts, enforces one member&#39;s current job/identity and sequences task dependencies; these are vertical task custody and S3 management, not an independent S2 policy/right for cross-worker disturbances. - Decision owner: constructor-authored Broker identity/attempt/dispatch predicates, not an autonomous peer coordination role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: attempt/job/request IDs, stale-attempt rejection, registered member binding, dependency-based dispatch, stored task status, ContextPacket permissions and revision handling. - Closure path: duplicate or outdated report/claim → Broker returns stale/conflict error or blocks wrong-version promotion → legitimate current attempt proceeds; no specific peer-pair source-write collision and returned inter-S1 remedy was evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: keeping attempt state consistent is real but chiefly current-task custody; neither message routing nor task ordering alone is enough to claim S2 closure. - Distinct S1 units: separately registered Pi or CLI workers assigned to independently scoped tickets/tasks under one approved project. - Inter-S1 disturbance: possible shared-folder edit collisions or conflicting dependency assumptions; shipped sources do not demonstrate systematic detection and resolution between these distinct workers. - Attenuating coordination relation: bounded task/attempt ownership and current-context protection; useful guards, but not source-supported lateral worker interference management. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: stale attempts are refused; this affects replacement attempts, not established peer workers negotiating or avoiding a particular cross-S1 collision. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: no S2-specific evidence survives the distinction, so positive S2 is withheld. - Evidence: pinned docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, apps/desktop/electron/bridgeBroker.cjs, apps/desktop/connectors/pi/worker.mjs. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high for inspected standard work path. - Caveats: a custom executor or human-specified workspace partition could prevent collisions; those arrangements are not shown to be a standard Luma-owned S2 feedback mechanism.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: a model-driven coordinator controls current ticket decomposition, assignment, review/rework and work-state changes under a user-approved plan version. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing work dependencies, failed worker attempts, insufficient acceptance evidence, delayed or lost executor heartbeats, partial plan revisions and stale reports across current tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect authoritative workbench, propose concrete scoped tasks and owners, invoke create-ticket/update-plan, connect or dispatch approved work, reject an exact report with reasons, or internally accept after review; decide what a returned failure requires within authorized project scope. - Decision owner: configured first-party agentcoordinator model using Luma&#39;s bounded MCP capability, while only the human owner can approve plan versions or grant final project acceptance. The Broker&#39;s static gate conditions remain constructor C support. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: xiaowuworkbench MCP, current snapshot, project-plan version, assignee constraints, scheduleCoordinatorWork, Broker mutations, attempt lineage, review/correction records and originating-conversation handoff. - Closure path: owner request / current snapshot → coordinator model selects plan, tasks, workers and corrective steps → user plan approval if required → Broker dispatch/worker execution → reports, artifacts, failures and reviews return → coordinator adjusts current work or returns exact findings, then escalates final approval to user. - Boundary reachability: apps/desktop/connectors/coordinator.mjs is a shipped model-facing tool; docs/PI.md and docs/ARCHITECTURE.md describe configured coordinator with actual workbench access; Broker authorizes the same endpoints. examples/coordinator-prompt.md supplies a supported coordinator role. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: coordinator model selects substantive decomposition and response to results through first-party-authorized tools. Broker validation is not the sole control decider, but neither can the model waive human plan/final acceptance. - Whole-system current view: approved project/plan revision, registered workers, task dependencies and statuses, active job/attempts, review reports, artifacts/checks, cancellations, rework and conversation-return status. - Current-control decision scope: present project execution, worker assignment and evidence-driven return; not future company portfolio strategy or constitutional identity. - Evidence: pinned apps/desktop/connectors/coordinator.mjs, apps/desktop/electron/bridgeBroker.cjs, docs/PI.md, docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, examples/coordinator-prompt.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for configured model coordinator and shipped current-control path. - Caveats: an API Profile that merely chats without an enabled coordinator/worker connection is not this supported S3=A mode. User approval requirements remain hard gates; controlled test fixtures do not measure autonomous model performance.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: separately from the implementing worker, a configured coordinator model challenges a submitted attempt against actual on-disk deliverables and acceptance checks, returning a concrete reject/repair verdict before internal acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: worker self-report that overstates completion, omitted/incorrect artifacts, missing test evidence and incomplete task acceptance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: call artifact on a submitted job/path, compare its contents and reported checks with task acceptance, and issue a binding reject-review with reason against exact attempt/report identifiers or internally accept if supported. - Decision owner: an independently invoked coordinator model/reviewer role (agentcoordinator), distinct from the registered Pi worker and from its output generation; Broker validates target identity and enforces review decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: coordinator workbench snapshot/artifact/reject-review/accept, read of approved artifact path via Broker, report/check/unknown metadata, exact attempt+report ID, preserved rejected evidence and repair/redispatch, final human acceptance. - Closure path: worker submits report and actual artifact → current ticket enters review → distinct coordinator accesses the submitted artifact itself and acceptance evidence → model challenges/accepts or rejects exact report with reasons → Broker returns rejected work for correction and keeps old evidence; eventual positive review still awaits human final completion. - Boundary reachability: the shipped coordinator MCP exposes artifact for authorized submitted text files, plus reject-review and accept; README/architecture/PI and examples/coordinator-prompt.md require the coordinator to examine actual artifacts and checks before acceptance. This is a conditional configured coordinator mode with actual tool use. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a different model-driven actor can inspect substantive file evidence and formulate case-specific negative findings; deterministic report-format checks or a purely summary-based verifier are not the source of the positive claim. - Claim being audited: a particular Pi/CLI worker&#39;s submitted deliverables meet task acceptance criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: implementing worker&#39;s summary, checks/unknowns and artifact list submitted via report. - Complementary access path: coordinator artifact reads up to 16 KB of the actual allowlisted text file inside the authorized workspace, rather than taking only the summary as proof; additional task/check context comes from authoritative snapshot. - Independence boundary: reviewer role and invocation are separate from worker, but it can use the same underlying model service and has only bounded artifact access; this is role/evidence-path independence, not guaranteed independent provider/human audit. - Who acts on findings: Broker checks exact current job/attempt/report and returns rejected task for repair, preserving prior evidence; accepted work advances to pending final human acceptance. - Evidence: pinned apps/desktop/connectors/coordinator.mjs, apps/desktop/electron/bridgeBroker.cjs (artifact, rejectReview, attachCoordinatorReviewer), examples/coordinator-prompt.md, docs/PI.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; auditor evidence access is supported but configuration/actual invocation dependent. - Caveats: the automatically spawned background coordinator review prompt currently provides report summary, check list and artifact names, not proof that it read the underlying files. Report-only automatic review by itself does not justify S3=A. This positive ownership applies only to the separately supported model coordinator mode that actually calls artifact; if that path is absent in a deployment the score must be narrowed. Synthetic tests do not prove empirical reviewer independence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no closed first-party future-capability or external-environment adaptation function beyond current request/plan handling was demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new owner goals, past conversation memory and proactive/diary features are contextual inputs or auxiliary product modes rather than supported anticipation of how Luma&#39;s organizational capability must change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: coordinator chooses present plan revisions within approved project scope, and humans can change member/API configuration; no prospectively accountable S4 actor updates the harness&#39;s long-term operating repertoire. - Decision owner: external user/maintainer for future mission and model/provider choices, with current coordinator for bounded work steps. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: saved history and optional private memory, activity schedules, revision logic, dynamic task creation, development roadmap/docs. - Closure path: new/changed task → current draft or amendment → user approval and execution; no first-party model of an outside future environment → capability proposal → authorized changed future organization state is demonstrated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model revising a project plan after errors is present-work S3, not itself a future-facing S4 adaptation mandate. - Evidence: pinned docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, docs/PI.md, apps/desktop/electron/bridgeBroker.cjs, README. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a human team using Luma could run an S4 practice with it, or optional memory plugins could learn; neither is borrowed into core first-party operating ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party ultimate identity/policy adjudication loop for the Luma work organization was found. - Disturbance / variety regulated: owner identity, member-role configuration, plan approval, final acceptance and workspace/API access are fixed or changed through human settings and constructor checks, not deliberated as constitutional organizational policy by an internal S5 actor. - Decisive decision or feedback right: only the human user may approve collaborative plans and finally accept the project; coordinator/model tokens are explicitly rejected for those endpoints. - Decision owner: external owner for defining identity/mission and final authorization; static Broker/host permission code for enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent agentowner versus agentcoordinator identities, authority mutation channel, explicit plan-version approval, final user-accept route, protected profiles/context, current grants and no chat-based privilege escalation. - Closure path: human project/permission decision → verified host/Broker state → bounded execution → final human acceptance; there is no ultimate identity/policy dispute internally adjudicated and returned as a new legitimate standing policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a review/plan gate and coordinator leadership are real S3 controls but not autonomous S5 ultimate-identity governance; even strongly enforced role identity is not a deliberative policy function. - Evidence: pinned docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, docs/PI.md, apps/desktop/electron/bridgeBroker.cjs, apps/desktop/connectors/coordinator.mjs. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: high for explicit user-gated policy boundary. - Caveats: a real owner organization may independently govern an OpenSiro-style policy plane outside Luma; this assessment does not credit it to the executable public harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1753063c228215c86965ea25c038cbda1571e154/assessments/lu/luma-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1753063c228215c86965ea25c038cbda1571e154\">1753063 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T13:46:33+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AtomUICommunity/TKCode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TKCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AtomUICommunity/TKCode/tree/620235e49fa0d7a1c7d80136a00414d9a398a102\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 620235e49fa0d7a1c7d80136a00414d9a398a102\">620235e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute source investigation, edits, commands and verification in response to a bounded delegated coding assignment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unexpected source structure, execution errors, incomplete specifications and changing worktree feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Built-in model selects tools, edits and next actions after seeing execution results. - Decision owner: Built-in Agent loop for default session and general-purpose worker loops in ACP. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool registry, workspace roots, file-state checks, JSONL session storage, permissions and model APIs. - Closure path: Agent calls tool; tool executes and returns feedback; model chooses its next coding action until it reports completion. - Boundary reachability: AgentHost creates built-in sessions and AgentCoordinator creates separate executable general-purpose subagent sessions; both run Agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool and action selection are runtime model decisions, not a fixed script. - Evidence: model/tool loop, AgentCoordinator.SpawnAsync, AgentTool.ExecuteAsync. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Upstream first-party source reviewed, without running the .NET desktop UI or external model.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate interference among coding workers operating on a shared codebase and interdependent change tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent edits to the same file and inconsistent dependent work across independently executing subagents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: ACP coordinator model decides whether tasks can run simultaneously based on non-overlapping file ownership, or must be sequential; can send corrective messages on conflict. - Decision owner: Parent model in first-party ACP role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Shared workspace and task registry, subagent names, spawn/report/message tools; per-session file mutation queues alone are insufficient cross-agent arbitration. - Closure path: Parent selects file-scoped assignments and parallel/sequential dispatch; receives agent reports; on conflict or gaps sends follow-up and changes subsequent worker tasks. - Boundary reachability: SessionRole.Acp is a selectable built-in role; SessionOptions.Apply installs the role and AgentTool/AgentCoordinator support actual first-party worker execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: File-overlap judgments and conflict resolution are instructions to a coordinating model; not merely mechanical shared task state. - Distinct S1 units: Separately instantiated general-purpose worker model loops, each editing its assigned source files. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Overlapping file mutations and dependent modifications that can invalidate another worker result. - Attenuating coordination relation: ACP instructs parent to serialize overlap and parallelize independent file ownership, plus resolve post-report conflicts. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Worker completion reports and post-hoc sendmessage cause parent to revise subagent tasks or execution sequence. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The explicit file-overlap interference rule and corrective feedback restrict concurrent worker actions for coherence, rather than merely distributing work. - Evidence: ACP role steps 3–7, AgentTool concurrency and messaging, Coordinator reports and notifications. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Source proves the model-facing ACP protocol and wired tools, not that every run successfully detects every overlapping write.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Manage the engagement&#39;s current task commitments, work assignment, integration and corrective interventions across executing workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incomplete delegation, blocked dependencies, failed implementation, inconsistent outcomes and integration gaps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: ACP coordinator designs current architecture, creates tasks, assigns files/workers, updates task commitments, reviews reports and dispatches corrective and final verification work. - Decision owner: First-party ACP coordinator model, with human user resolving scope-changing choices and approving tool calls as needed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Shared TaskList, taskcreate/taskupdate/tasklist, AgentCoordinator, worker reports and inherited permission gates. - Closure path: Parent sets engagement task plan and owners, receives worker outcomes, revises tasks/assignments, orders corrections and final verification before reporting completion. - Boundary reachability: Built-in ACP role restricts coordinator tools, shared tasks are wired via AgentHost, and workers inherit parent task list in AgentCoordinator.SpawnAsync. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model makes task prioritization and corrective resource/commitment decisions across the worker system rather than mechanically enforcing a budget or timeout. - Whole-system current view: Parent ACP model&#39;s task list, assigned file boundaries, architecture plan and returned agent reports. - Current-control decision scope: Task decomposition, agent assignment, dependency/parallel sequencing, rework and final integration assurance. - Evidence: ACP controlling instructions, task management API, shared work/task registration. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: S3 is established for the optional ACP coding engagement, not for a project-wide institution or ordinary one-agent session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No separately owned complementary audit channel challenges the parent engagement management independently. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Defects or omissions in worker outputs can trigger routine verification, but independence is not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The same coordinator may ask a subagent to run tests/review; no independent auditor owns a privileged S3-to-S3 challenge right. - Decision owner: ACP parent delegates verification and evaluates worker reports. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ACP step 7 verification delegation, test/command tools, AgentTool reports and task records. - Closure path: Verification reports return through the ordinary delegated-task channel into the same parent, not an independent complementary audit channel. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A verification subagent is a worker appointed and scoped by the same plan owner; this is normal S3 quality checking unless separate access/independent challenge and corrective accountability are shown. - Evidence: ACP review/verification instructions, subagent report delivery, task tools. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A human could arrange independent review outside the shown standard runtime; not credited here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No demonstrated prospective environmental/capability adaptation beyond executing the current coding assignment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unforeseen code and user requirements trigger local planning and replanning, not harness-wide future adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: ACP model plans the present implementation and handles source contingencies; it does not own an external-future capability strategy. - Decision owner: Agent/ACP parent for current work; developer/operator for future TKCode capability changes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan role, askuser, task creation, model/runtime selection and user-configured roles. - Closure path: Task plans feed current implementation, not an external-capability proposal into a distinct S3 management decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A code implementation plan or selectable model is not an organizational future/environment intelligence right. - Evidence: Plan/ACP role, session roles/options, README. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Real product roadmapping is outside the scoped runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No autonomous or function-specific parent runtime identity/ultimate-policy governance is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User instructions, security permissions and safety rules affect tool execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human approves or refuses individual actions; built-in modes and permissions enforce preset limits, not legitimate policy-level adjudication. - Decision owner: User/maintainers for identity and policies; code restricts tool scope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Ask/autoApprove/fullAccess, Plan approvals, role filters, prompt limits and workspace permission checks. - Closure path: Tool approval gates an individual action or plan; no identity-level conflict moves through ultimate authority and returns as revised governing policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Operator approval and static permission modes do not establish an S5 policy decision/feedback organization at engagement recursion. - Evidence: session permission modes, session approval bridge, role filters. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Corporate or repository governance should not be inferred from runtime UI controls.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/fd4915af9d488ef8b7aa4fc98764a8d5406c8e5d/assessments/tk/tkcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit fd4915af9d488ef8b7aa4fc98764a8d5406c8e5d\">fd4915a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T23:56:39+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dohwi/pipaya\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pipaya</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dohwi/pipaya/tree/dd5cdfd560c1499301a1f2fe4c07f9173a12d53a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dd5cdfd560c1499301a1f2fe4c07f9173a12d53a\">dd5cdfd &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Persistent model-backed workspace sessions perform coding/investigation tasks and can use disposable helpers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User requests, file/command results, provider faults, context pressure and delegated observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose workspace tools, follow-up actions and final answers; choose whether to delegate or create a persistent peer. - Decision owner: The configured session agent, composed by the first-party SessionManager around Pi Agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pi inference/loop dependency, first-party tools, SQLite transcript/context state, queues, compaction, retry and limits. - Closure path: Prompt → constructed Agent with owned tool definitions → selected calls → observations/persistence → further turns and final response. - Boundary reachability: ensureLive creates Agent with the session&#39;s actual model, history, prompt and first-party tools. The packaged server and desktop-bundled server wire this composition directly. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The first-party product owns the concrete executable composition; Pi SDK is a dependency and does not donate uninstantiated higher functions. Without the session actor, server APIs and gates do not choose project work. - Evidence: [Session construction and helper execution][R1], [tools][R2], [server distribution][R3], [manifest][R4]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: No provider-backed E2E run or Pi-wide capability claim is made.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: The supplied peer protocol attenuates reciprocal reply/notification oscillation between independently running sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two peer sessions can repeatedly reply to one another or cause completion notifications to trigger further replies. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The receiving agent decides whether a peer response is needed and whether to end that exchange; the sender chooses explicit reply/notification requests. - Decision owner: The participating session agents own semantic response necessity; runtime-authored notification and send bounds constrain the exchange. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: peerMessage marks other-session origin and says to reply only if needed; completion watches are consumed once, reports say no reply needed, and per-run send overflow returns stop/report feedback. - Closure path: Peer message or one-shot completion report → recipient agent sees explicit reply/termination semantics → chooses further peersend only when needed, or stops; cap refusal feeds back to the sender&#39;s next action. - Boundary reachability: Peer tools are automatically included in normal session tool plans unless the operator disables them. PeerService wraps actual delivered messages and sends report feedback into prompt/followup paths of ordinary agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The narrow credited discretion is agent choice over necessary replies under a first-party anti-oscillation protocol. The generic mailbox, layout groups and delegation are not themselves the S2 witness, and the counter is not an autonomous owner. - Evidence: [Peer templates, send counter and one-shot watchers][R5], [tool descriptions][R2], [always-reachable peer tool wiring][R1], [tool plan][R6]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: This is bounded structural attenuation, not proof that arbitrary agent conversations terminate. The counter resets each run and cannot alone rule out cross-run ping-pong or shared-file collisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: A session agent can regulate current commitments across peer sessions, and the local human operator has a distinct supplied supervisory mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Peers are stuck, working on the wrong course, waiting on questions, or consuming the wrong model/active-session capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect all peer state, steer/abort work, create/close/resume sessions, withdraw queued work, change model/effort and answer blocked questions; in parent mode the operator makes those current-control choices through the server/client API. - Decision owner: Base mode: a model-backed session acting as peer supervisor. Parent mode: the local server operator using the administrator-authority client/API. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: peerlist/read/current watcher notifications, peersend steer/followup, peerabort/manage/create/answer, persisted session state and authenticated REST/WebSocket interfaces. - Closure path: Current peer view/report → agent or operator selects intervention → SessionManager applies queue/run/model/lifecycle/question changes → subsequent peer execution follows the changed current commitment. - Boundary reachability: Every normal session receives the first-party peer tools, wired to SessionManager host methods, unless disabled. The client/API exposes server-wide activity/session/status/usage and actual abort/message/patch/question endpoints for its operator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the supervisory actor leaves APIs, messages and limits but no discretionary selection of whom to stop, steer, resume or reconfigure. Parent control is evidenced separately from generic askuser approval. - Evidence: [Peer state/intervention methods][R5], [specific tool semantics][R2], [SessionManager host wiring][R1], [authenticated operator routes][R7], [server API contract][R3]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Authority is broad among sessions of one trusted server; this is neither a global organizational claim nor evidence that every run uses a supervisor.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No shipped audit-specific independent challenge-and-corrective-control loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A peer or helper may make an incorrect completion claim. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No supplied audit role/verdict/return protocol decides that claim independently. - Decision owner: Ordinary producing sessions report their work. Helper role files and extension logic are user supplied. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: peerread can inspect ordinary transcript/tool snippets; workspace read/grep and generic helpers are available; event/context/read tracing preserve evidence. - Closure path: Peer/helper output returns as ordinary tool text or messages, without an audit-specific independently controlled adjudication and corrective path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A fresh helper context and raw file tools can be used to build a reviewer, but the shipped delegate interface itself is generic operational delegation. No bundled reviewer is credited from an external marketplace. - Evidence: [Helper definition/execution][R8], [session delegate][R1], [peer read][R5], [extension boundary][R9]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Users can create a material complementary audit application; it needs its own independent owner, evidence-access and corrective-return proof.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No external-and-prospective adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider configuration, new skills/extensions, changing instructions and available upstream releases. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party intelligence path develops future-relevant options and negotiates a return into current capability. - Decision owner: Users configure endpoints/extensions/skills and request updates; sessions perform requested present tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AGENTS.md/skill refresh, compaction, provider settings, extension APIs and release-tag updater. - Closure path: Settings apply next run; updater installs the latest release after operator request and running-session checks. No prospective environment model/adaptation-option decision loop is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A latest-tag inequality check and package restart are maintenance, not external/future adaptation judgment. General capability extension and compaction are not S4; no parent adaptation mode is inferred from update consent. - Evidence: [Source update policy][R10], [live context/skills][R11], [SessionManager][R1], [extension skill boundary][R9]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: The assessment does not claim that users cannot organize an adaptive workflow on this product.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity/ultimate-policy adjudication-and-return loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Session instructions, permissions, enabled skills/tools and broad server administrator control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No identity-level issue is routed to ultimate authority and returned as an authoritative system policy decision. - Decision owner: Users author prompts/settings; trusted extensions run with server privileges; agents can change bounded peer metadata/current work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Authentication token, frozen initial tool/base-prompt snapshot, live AGENTS/skill settings, extension approval and per-tool enforcement. - Closure path: Settings constrain subsequent calls, and askuser/peeranswer returns ordinary question answers. Those are not a supplied identity-policy adjudication protocol. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A field named purpose, a user answer and administrator control are not S5 without a function-specific ultimate-policy issue and governing return. - Evidence: [Context and instruction boundary][R11], [session controls][R1], [question lifecycle][R12], [operator API][R7], [peer metadata][R5]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: The S3 parent mode is not reused as S5 evidence; external extension policy does not enter the first-party default boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/af57467e7adc84c630a0ffc6bb9cfb46e586a39f/assessments/pi/pipaya.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit af57467e7adc84c630a0ffc6bb9cfb46e586a39f\">af57467 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T21:17:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rokartur/agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agent (rokartur)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rokartur/agent/tree/f02a473edd8a08368876e45a641f8ed8c2a4c85d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f02a473edd8a08368876e45a641f8ed8c2a4c85d\">f02a473 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: coding transformation by root and child agent loops - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown source, tool failures, changing files and delegated findings - Decisive decision or feedback right: select tool/action/arguments and revise the approach from results - Decision owner: root/child models within their assigned scopes - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, permissions, tool dispatcher, JSONL persistence, snapshots and channels - Closure path: model tool choice → execution result → next model input; child answer/follow-up → root continuation - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model leaves execution support but no discretionary solution. - Evidence: source/src/agent/mod.rs#L539-L670, source/src/agent/subagent.rs#L188-L215, source/src/agent/subagent.rs#L263-L323 - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Pinned source establishes a supported path, not observed effectiveness or universal deployment. - Boundary reachability: Agent::runturns is called by supported CLI/server modes; child launch uses the current installed executable.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent stale writes from overwriting another operational writer&#39;s changes - Disturbance / variety regulated: root/parallel children can act on the same cwd after reading different file versions - Decisive decision or feedback right: reject stale anchored edits and return fresh-read feedback; broader coordination discretion still needs composition - Decision owner: first-party anchor controller enforces the rule; no separately assigned autonomous cross-writer coordination owner - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session-specific anchors, reread/sync before edit, model-visible error and common child cwd - Closure path: another writer changes file → stale anchor rejected → caller receives ESTALEANCHOR and must reread before a valid subsequent edit - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A concrete collision-specific path exists; deterministic rejection does not itself own autonomous coordination judgment. C credits this narrow supplied path, not the ability to program any coordinator. - Evidence: source/src/tools/hashline.rs#L308-L329, source/src/tools/hashline.rs#L654-L659, source/src/agent/mod.rs#L975-L1004, source/src/agent/mod.rs#L640-L666 - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Pinned source establishes a supported path, not observed effectiveness or universal deployment. - Boundary reachability: Hashline-backed tools are installed for the coding agents and child launch defaults to the root cwd. - Distinct S1 units: root coding agent and parallel child coding agents. - Inter-S1 disturbance: stale read-modify-write on shared files after a different writer edits them. - Attenuating coordination relation: anchor ownership plus reread/sync rejects stale range rather than silently overwriting. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: caller receives explicit fresh-read instruction in tool result, then chooses its next action. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited path is version-sensitive writer-conflict rejection, not child spawning, work ordering or a mailbox.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: current cohesion and accountability of the bounded child task team - Disturbance / variety regulated: different child states, failed/superseded work and model/effort commitments - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect team roster/results, change active child scope, stop and resume commitments, select model/effort for replacement work - Decision owner: root model for its current team - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ListAgents, SendMessage, TaskStop, subagent state and follow-up queues; static concurrency cap is enforcement - Closure path: team status/result → root current judgment → steering/stop/resume/new scoped resource choice → changed child operations → later state/result - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This is narrower than global fleet governance and exceeds initial decomposition: a running child can be steered, stopped and resumed after accountability feedback. - Evidence: source/src/agent/mod.rs#L892-L1029, source/src/agent/subagent.rs#L92-L108, source/src/tools/tools.json, source/src/agent/swarm-prompt.md#L18-L35 - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Task-team scope only. Generic human plan approval, session interruption and permission-mode edits are not credited as a separate parent S3 mode. - Boundary reachability: Root&#39;s shipped tool set exposes status and intervention; follow-ups enter its continuing run loop. No custom control-plane code is required. - Whole-system current view: root&#39;s roster of its child tasks/status plus returned answers, within the task-team recursion. - Current-control decision scope: ongoing team commitments, task scope, model/effort resources and intervention; not provider infrastructure or unrelated sessions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge delegated outcome claims with complementary primary-artifact access - Disturbance / variety regulated: a child answer can be mistaken despite appearing complete - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently open cited source or rerun the decisive command before relying on the child&#39;s claim - Decision owner: root agent as a decision holder distinct from the child producer - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in swarm guidance embedded in Agent tool description, root Read/Bash, returned child claim and root continuation - Closure path: child answer → root selects checks on depended-on claims → raw artifact/command evidence → root revises or withholds next work and can steer/resume the child - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The shipped instruction explicitly treats the answer as a claim and names an alternate evidence path; judgment resides with the root, not a deterministic hook. This does not rely on naming an arbitrary spawned child &#39;auditor&#39;. - Evidence: source/src/agent/swarm-prompt.md#L18-L27, source/src/tools/mod.rs#L38-L43, source/src/agent/mod.rs#L1022-L1029, source/src/agent/mod.rs#L892-L920 - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Prompt-guided supported audit mode, not evidence that every run performs it. Auto permission reviewer and deep-research&#39;s mandatory verify phase are not used to establish complementary audit. - Boundary reachability: The default bundled swarm prompt is injected into the root&#39;s Agent tool description; root already has source/command tools and consumes child results in supported modes. - Claim being audited: child&#39;s completion/finding claims on which the root&#39;s next step depends. - Ordinary reporting path: child final answer or queued background follow-up. - Complementary access path: root independently reads cited path:line or reruns decisive command against workspace reality. - Independence boundary: root context/judgment is separate from the child production context; model provider may be shared, so this is workflow/claim independence, not model-diversity assurance. - Who acts on findings: root changes its own next step or sends corrective child instructions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: outside-and-then adaptation of the harness/team capability - Disturbance / variety regulated: Web research, long-term goals and self-update are not by themselves prospective system adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive path established. - Decision owner: None established for this function in the assessed mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bundled deep-research/workflows, goal/todo/cron, model routing, compaction, update and skills - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific return into operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deep-research produces the requested research report; updates follow published versions. No shipped loop turns external prospective distinctions into adaptation options and a current-capability regulatory return; parent adaptation was not established. - Evidence: source/src/agent/deep-research.js, source/src/update.rs, source/src/tools/goal.rs, source/src/agent/workflow.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: identity / ultimate-policy closure - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ask/Auto/FullAccess permissions, trust and prompt policy bound ordinary tool actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive path established. - Decision owner: None established for this function in the assessed mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission reviewer, trusted project instructions, plan approval, settings, root swarm constraints and session UI - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific return into operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The review judges an individual proposed action against the existing request; it neither resolves identity-policy tension nor constructs an authoritative policy-return loop. Mutable prompts and contributor governance do not close S5 for the deployed task team. - Evidence: source/src/agent/permissions.rs#L178-L229, source/src/agent/mod.rs#L1106-L1141, source/src/agent/prompt.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/28430b0a3ee80e366fca884d46873921a7deb469/assessments/ag/agent-rokartur.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 28430b0a3ee80e366fca884d46873921a7deb469\">28430b0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
